CinefexSearchData.register("text/12",{"issue":12,"date":"April 1983","pages":[["1","Cover","number 12 $4.75 Canada - $5.95 cine-ex"],["2","Closing pages","DARKS PARDEMONIUM CARNIVAL ST ANTHONY'S TEMPLE OF TEMPTATION FREAKS ROUND ABOUT WILD ANIMAL JUNGLE"],["3","Contents","cinefex CINEFEX 12 - April 1983. Published quarterly at P.O. Box 20027, Riverside, California 92516. Subscription rates (U.S. only): four issues - $12; ten issues - $25. Return postage must accompany all unsolicited manuscripts, photos or other materials. Printed by Valley Printers, Sunnymead, California; typesetting by The Set-Up, Riverside, California; color separations by ViviColour, City of Industry, California. Contents copyright © 1983 by Don Shay. All rights reserved. Reproduction in whole or in part without permission is prohibited. ...the journal of cinematic illusions editor & publisher Don Shay contributing editors Paul Mandell Jordan Fox circulation manager JoDyne Shay editorial consultant Robert P. Everett editorial services Ann Dredla Something Wicked This Way Comes - Adding the Magic.. Culminating a gestation period which spanned nearly a quarter of a century, Ray Bradbury's chilling fantasy novel, Something Wicked This Way Comes, has at last materialized on the screen under the Disney banner. Ray Bradbury and director Jack Clayton discuss the origin and evolution of the project, while effects supervisor Lee Dyer - assisted by seven key department heads - details the elaborate mechanical and optical effects, makeup, miniatures, matte paintings and computer animation that transformed the film in postproduction. Article by Brad Munson. Stop-Frame Fever, Post-Animation Blues. After a short-lived resurgence in Clash of the Titans and Caueman, the venerable old art of stop motion animation has slipped once again into the role of supporting performer, struggling for a foothold among the superstar effects technologies of the day. Animators David Allen, Randall William Cook and Steven Archer discuss their respective efforts - successful and otherwise - at adding stop-motion moments to such recent and forthcoming productions as The Howling, Q, The Thing, The Hunger and Krull. Article by Paul Mandell. A Dream in the Making. From humble beginnings in a residential garage, Dream Quest has grown - in three short years - into one of the most highly respected effects facilities in the business. A close partnership comprised of six predominantly young, but seasoned professionals, the Dream Quest princi pals - Scott Squires, Rocco Gioffre, Hoyt Yeatman, Robert Hollister, Tom Hollister and Frederick Iguchi - recount their evolution as a company, their ancillary work on such projects as Escape from New York, E.T. and One from the Heart, their first solo outing on Blue Thunder, and their prospects for the future. Article by Marc A. Richardson. FRONT COVER - The evil Dust Witch from Something Wicked This Way Comes. The giant crystal spider approaches her cocoon in Krull - BACK COVER. 4 28 .50"],["4","Something Wicked This Way Comes","Something Makerk This Way Comes Adding the Magic NIUM FANDE A"],["5","Something Wicked This Way Comes","\"It's about my childhood, actually. I wanted to do a book that would scare me the way life and death scared me when I was a certain age. When you're lying in bed late at night. When you see shadows in your closet, and hear someone walking around in your attic, and there should be no one up there. That's what it's about. That's beautiful, that's mysterious, that's evocative.\" ...and that is the heart of Ray Bradbury's Something Wicked This Way Comes, the strange and subtle fantasy of two boys who battle an evil carnival and the creatures that inhabit it. After almost a quarter of a century, and after innumerable nearmisses and false starts in the hands of producers and directors ranging from Gene Kelly to Sam Peckinpah, Something Wicked This Way Comes finally found a home at Walt Disney Productions. But Something Wicked experienced a long history of change and evolution - one even more lengthy and complicated than the usual long-term film project. And its final version displays a unique blend of special effects technologies that, oddly enough, were brought to bear only after the film itself was virtually completed. It begins with Ray Bradbury himself, an acclaimed writer of novels, short stories and screenplays. \"Wicked actually started twenty-four years ago, when I went to a screening of Invitation to the Dance at M-G-M. Gene Kelly had asked me to come over and see the film, and I went with my wife and some friends. I loved the picture. I came out in tears, saying: 'I'd love to work with Gene Kelly.' I went home that night, touched by the picture, and I started going through my files, seeing if I could find something I could take to him. I found this short story called 'Black Ferris,' which was in Weird Tales thirty years or so ago, and I said: 'You know, I've often thought of turning that into a longer novella. Why don't I do an outline on it and take it to Kelly?' \" Bradbury spent a week creating a seventy- or eighty-page outline, called Dark Carnival, which he hand-delivered to Kelly's door. Kelly said: 'Wow, this is terrific I want to direct it. Can I have it? I'll take it to Europe and try to get some money.' I said: 'Fine. It's yours, free. Take it.' So he went off to Europe - I don't know how long he was gone - and finally he came back and said: 'It's no use. No one cares. I can't get the money.' I said: 'Well, the heck with it. I'll write it as a novel.'\" Over the next two years, on and off, Bradbury completed the Dark Carnival story as a novel, under the title Something Wicked This Way Comes. The book sold steadily from its first publication in 1962 - to adults as well as children - and since that long-ago day, the property has fallen into the hands of more prospective producers than Bradbury can conveniently recall.iWe talked to a variety of directors, including Sam Peckinpah at one time, and Mark Rydell, and even Spielberg. At one time or another all were interested, all promised, and all disappeared, for whatever reasons. But finally the property came back into my hands, and Something Wicked This Way Comes photographs copyright © 1983 by Walt Disney Productions. World rights reserved. Still photography by Mel Traxel. Special thanks to Howard Green. about six or seven years ago it started all over again - this time with Kirk Douglas and Peter, his son.\" It was during this period that Bradbury discussed the project with his friend Jack Clayton, whom he had known since working with him on John Huston's Moby Dick, for which Bradbury wrote the screenplay. A soft-spoken and gentle Englishman, Clayton's films - which include The Innocents, The Pumpkin Eater, and the little-known but pleasantly bizarre Our Mother's House - have been primarily psychological dramas, though in the United States he is probably best known for his lush but still understated adaptation ofF. Scott Fitzgerald's The Great Gatsby. Clayton shared Bradbury's enthusiasm for the project, and when Douglas struck a deal with Paramount to produce the film he was one of the first on board. \"I'm always looking for something different,\" Clayton said. \"I don'tlike repeating myself. And I realized six years ago...what about fantasy? I had various - not rules, exactly - but thoughts on how you would make a fantasy. And I got hooked, as I often do.\" But once again, Something Wicked encountered difficulties. Political problems at the studio halted the preproduction effort after no more than eight months; and only in 1980, when Disney vice president Tom Willhite took on the project, was Douglas able to reunite the team of Bradbury and Clayton. But the effort was far from over. \"I spent six months doing a whole new screenplay for Jack,\" said Bradbury, \"which was a gorgeous experience. Jack is a wonderful man to sit with every afternoon.\" At first, Bradbury delivered an immense script - about 250 pages long - and only with Clayton's gentle insistence did he begin to edit it.There are two arts,\" said Bradbury, after the painful process was completed. \"Getting a thing done, and then learning how to cut it so you don't kill it or hurt it in any way. When you start out life as a writer, you hate that job; but now that I'm older, it's turned into a wonderful game, and I love the challenge just as much as writing the original.\" Eventually, a cast was chosen. Jason Robards would play Charles Halloway, the melancholy librarian who fights the evil carnival despite his own guilt and mounting despair. Jonathan Pryce, a Tony Award-winning stage actor, was signed on as a surprisingly young, dapper and consummately evil Mr. Dark - leader of the Dark Carnival and a living representation of \"the night and death and age and all the things that tempt us to misbehave.\" Pam Grier, a stunning black actress, became not only the Dust Witch, but also the Most Beautiful Woman in the World and virtually every other female member of the carnival. And the special innocence and atmosphere of the story would be preserved by placing the small Midwestern village of Green Town in \"that timeless age between the late 1920s and the early 1930s.\" Finally, early in 1981, principal photography began - without the benefit of a complete special effects crew. Effects supervisor Lee Dyer, who was not to become involved in the project until much later, explained why. \"We had EPCOT, which was one of article by the biggest undertakings of this studio. We had Tron. We had Brad Munson Tex. For months, this was the busiest studio in Hollywood. And"],["6","Something Wicked This Way Comes","Bradbury looks altogether at library set constructed for the film adaptation of his fantasy classic, Something Wicked This Way Comes. / After more than six years of intermittent preproduction, veteran film director Jack Clayton was given the opportunity to see the project through to fruition on the Disney lot. / Due to overcommitment on the part input. Once edited, however, something more was needed, so effects supervisor Lee Dyer (right) was engaged to muster the forces necessary to \"add enhanced scene with effects Novelist-screenwriter Ray home in the atmospheric of the Disney effects organization, Something Wicked went all the way through principal photography without significant special effects it became clear that magic\" to the film. Dyer reviews an animationliaison Clint Colver. • CINEFEX 12 then we had Something Wicked going through - which was a major film, too. Unfortunately, we just couldn't accommodate everyone, so Wicked had nobody to coordinate the effects for them. Clayton, from the very beginning, had asked for special effects to be put in the film - but no one was available.\" Clayton himself agreed. \"Three-quarters of the way through I was bleating, saying: 'Where are the special effects?' And they, equally, were trying to put me off - rightly so, because they had to finish Tron and EPCOT.\" Consequently, Jack Clayton, virtually undisturbed by Disney's vast special effects staff, went about shooting his personal vision of Something Wicked This Way Comes. Principal photography was completed early in 1982, and Clayton oversaw what he thought would be a final cut. Then and only then did Disney executives call in the special effects experts - first and foremost Lee Dyer, who was just completing frantic work on Tron as special effects animation supervisor. An old Disney hand since Mary Poppins, and a respected freelancer who had returned to the studio especially for Tron, Dyer had been asked by Tom Willhite to offer his opinions on the possibilities that animation might present in \"adding magic\" to Clayton's film. That phrase became the central concern of everyone involved in the project, from Clayton and Bradbury to Dyer and the entire effects crew. Here was a story that Disney was counting on - based on a well-loved book that had breathed magic for three generations of readers. So Dyer agreed to take a look and make suggestions in the last week of May, just before leaving for a well-deserved vacation. \"I gave him five pages of notes of what I thought could be done on this picture,\" Dyer recalled later, covering all avenues from the beginning to the end. At first, when Jack Clayton was told that we were going back in - in fact, doing these five pages of notes - I guess he just about went through the ceiling. I can imagine. If it had been my film, and if I'd spent that much time and energy on it and thought that basically I was done with it...well, you can imagine how you would feel if someone came in and suggested incredible changes. But as we talked about them, and showed Jack the boards - as we brought him in on every, every board that was done and took his suggestions and worked together - the more he got involved, the more he was enthused about the cut and what we could do. Jack had wanted this all along. He would have welcomed it in the first place. It's just that we in effects were totally tied up.\" The first step to \"adding the magic\" was storyboarding the film from start to finish. \"I'm a firm believer in boards,\" Dyer explained. ¿'Especially now, when films are so expensive to make. If you take six months and pin down, if nothing else, the action sequences or the sequences that are vague in the story, it more than pays for itself. It makes the shooting go faster: and apart from the budget, it's a way of looking at the continuity - if things are working or not. You can build suspense, and you can certainly build camera angles.\" Clayton, however, had never worked with special effects before - or with storyboards. \"I don't like them,\" he commented. \"It inhibits what you can do on the set. But I recognize that you have to have storyboards for special effects. So Lee Dyer and I went over the film together, and then he and his staff produced storyboards. I corrected them in some places, but basically it's his special effects.\" Dyer and his crew had a happy surprise, too.iWe soon discovered as we worked together that everything we proposed to do, Clayton had thought of doing, but hadn't known how to go about it. He just wasn't a special effects man, and he's the first to admit it.\" The reshooting that Dyer eventually supervised and Clayton directed was done entirely with storyboards as guides. \"We shot fourteen hours a day, and we could go from one setup to another immediately, because we had the boards. It was just merely the mechanics of putting the thing together and shooting it. We shot an average of eight hundred to a thousand feet a day, sometimes a little more, and almost every frame was used.\" So what began as a series of suggestions to merely enhance bits and pieces of the existing film quickly grew to something wider and more fundamental. \"Initially,\" Dyer said, \"it wasn't really 'Let's change this sequence' or 'Let's do this to enhance the story line' or anything like that. It was basically enhancing the overall look with animation. But then as we got into it, we found that we had real problems with this area or that area not quite coming off as good as it should. We had plot and character problems.\" There was Tom Fury, for instance - the itinerant lightning rod salesman who opens the film. \"In the original cut,\" Dyer continued, Fury wasn't quite clear. He just sort of vaguely showed up in town. What I proposed was that Fury was ahead of the storm - always warning the townspeople that this storm was coming, that the Dust People were coming. So now we open the story with Tom Fury coming down the road, and he looks over his shoulder to see what we call 'Wicked Clouds' behind him, very far in the distance. As the story progresses, these clouds build up in intensity, and instead of most pictures where the storm is the evil force, in this picture it's the good force - the Hand of God - and at the very end, the storm centers over the carnival itself and literally sucks it up into the sky. Everything is lost - the townspeople who have become involved with the carnival, everybody in the carnival, Dark - everything is finally eradicated off the face of the earth, so they can no longer come back as they have in the past.\" Soon Clayton and Dyer realized that they would be calling on the full resources of the studio, and on several new and expanding technologies - some of which would be entirely new to Disney films. First, Dyer pulled back most of the key people who had been involved in Tron. A staff of fifteen was assembled. Clint Colver, one of the scene coordinators on Tron, served as liaison between the animators and Disney's process lab, helping composite more than 175 different optical effects. Jesse Silver, one of the key background painters on Tron, also returned. Robert J. Schiffer, one of the few Disney technicians involved in the original shoot, stayed on to lend his Academy Award-winning skills as makeup artist. Harrison Ellenshaw was asked to super-"],["7","Something Wicked This Way Comes","CINEFEX 12 • 7"],["8","Something Wicked This Way Comes","8 - CINFFEX 12"],["9","Something Wicked This Way Comes","vise the construction of miniatures, along with Ron Tantin, head of Disney's special effects department. Recent additions to the organization were also called - Isadore Raponi, a long-time associate of Carlo Rambaldi, worked on the miniatures, and designed and built the extraordinary mechanical spiders used in one special sequence. Richard Portman, another Oscar winner, for sound work on The Deer Hunter, brought his own boundless enthusiasm and Disney's new digital sound studio to bear on the They were all there to add the magic. The entire preproduction team, Clayton and Bradbury included, began with the footage that already existed. \"Basically,\" said Dyer, \"we looked at the film and asked: 'What can we do with this? Can we use existing footage? If not, will we cut it or use new footage?' \" Essentially, the fully storyboarded film was broken down into four parts: 1) sequences to be discarded entirely; 2) existing sequences to be enhanced through opticals, matte work, re-editing or some other means; 3) existing sequences to be reshot in part or in their entirety; and 4) entirely new sequences to replace cut footage or make new places for themselves in the picture. \"All in all,\" Dyer said, \"we added probably twenty or twenty-five minutes to the film.\" The first category - the discarded or \"lost\" sequences - make a fascinating record of how Something Wicked came to be. Some of the cut footage simply did not come up to standards; other cuts were made purely as a matter of style. Clayton, characteristically, had attempted to create suspense through atmosphere, and occasionally through ambiguity, Consequently, certain sequences never fully hooked up with the overall narrative. At one point in the film, the two boys, Will Halloway and Jim Nightshade, skulk about Dark's circus and encounter a dimly-lit tent. In the final version of the film, they find Tom Fury inside, strapped to a chair. But in the original version, they found something more - a large group of fully-realized circus freaks that ranged from the bizarre to the hideous. Originally, Bob Schiffer had let his imagination run wild in producing the freaks. \"I had a bird-lady - a whole appliance down from the forehead to the nose and mouth, with electric hair. There were midgets with huge domes; and we bought thirty or forty very expensive manikins, and I did a character on each one. They were dressed in crazy costumes and interspersed with the midgets and other people that were also freaks. It was a kind of frightening thing when the boys walked into it. But I think it probably wasn't covered well. It didn't mean much when they shot it, and it didn't fit in.\" Dyer agreed. Clayton had certain ideas about the freak tent, and I disagreed with them - totally. I wanted to go back and shoot it again, to make these very frightening freaks instead of what was in the film. But the cost was too extreme. The only thing we really had to save was Fury being electrocuted; so now the boys are attracted by Dark and Cooger talking, and they go into the tent with the flap opening by itself. Inside, they see Fury is in the chair: and a moment later, the electrocution begins.\" Fury's electrocution, too, has changed from its original form. Many of the magical elements in the first cut were eventually replaced as the postproduction effects unit swung into action. Working over an unrecognizably altered Pam Grier, hair stylist Edie Panda adds final touches to a makeup creation by Robert Schiffer. Grier's sinister Dust Witch, normally beautiful, had to be altered to her true form for a brief onscreen appearance, and Schiffer developed several different concepts, none of which proved satisfactory. I Schiffer removes the makeup appliance. / Mechanical effects technicians Clay Pinney, Pat Domenico and Curt Dickson operate a large mechanical hand - another early element deleted from the final film. / Actors Shawn Carson and Vidal I. Peterson react in fear as the giant hand scratches at their bedroom window. / Lee Dyer and Jack Clayton confer between setups in the mirror maze. 9 CINEFEX 12"],["10","Something Wicked This Way Comes","Animators Scott Russo and Clint Colver one of the pivotal scenes in the library where youth. As he metaphorically rips pages from a book, each was achieved entirely through postproduction animation. / Gale Fox, working from frame interactive lighting effects. / generated for those areas of normally be expected to glow if the pages actually flared. / (Jonathan Pryce) rips a page view reveals both the glowing Michael Wolf discuss with Mr. Dark tempts Halloway with promises of restored leaf flares with a startling unearthly glow. The effect enlargements of Jason Robards, determines appropriate placement of Airbrush artwork was the image which would With a flourish, Dark from the book. / A closer page and the interactive lighting on Dark's face. 10 CINEFEX 12 For the current version, Lee Dyer and his crew animated a series of frighteningly powerful lightning bolts. But at one point, long before the special effects crew was called in, a Tesla static electricity generator was actually constructed to produce the effect. \"They made a tin-foil dummy of Fury,\" Clint Colver recalled, set him in his chair and hooked him up to a Tesla generator. They got static electricity going on him, and did a number of takes on it. But for some reason, the real thing ended up looking worse than animation. It was too much - it was all over him. With animation, you have much more control. We could locate sparks at the switch, and on the manacles of his hands, and fit it all in with the shots better.\" Dyer agreed, rejecting the Tesla footage outright. \"They'd spent a great deal of time and money building it and shooting it and trying to put it in over the live-action. Of course it didn't work. Then they went to an outside animation house and somebody there animated it - I think, four different times - and the effects were absolutely atrocious. The electricity crawled all over Fury in lines. It was terrible. The electrocution effects were among the first things to be redone on the Disney lot. Meanwhile, there was the affair of the mechanical hand - another sequence that had gone through a number of changes. In Bradbury's original story, the two boys are stalked by the evil and hideously ugly Dust Witch, who sends out huge snails to mark the roof of their houses so she can find them when she floats above Green Town in her hot air balloon. But that image was eliminated even before the first script was completed. Bradbury was disappointed, but he took it well. \"Everyone seemed afraid to try to fly the Dust Witch over the house; and past a certain point you have to respect your director, and say: 'Okay, what else can we do where you'll feel more secure?' There's no way to guarantee that, if we did it my way, it would work out and not turn us into laughing stocks.\" The second attempt, then, was to design and build a huge mechanical hand, something on the order of the famous ape's paw in King Kong. The boys would huddle in their bedroom, listening to heavy footfalls scratching on the roof - and then see the giant fingers scrabbling at the window. Once again, the idea simply didn't work. \"The mechanical hand looked absurd,\" Dyer remarked. \"Jack Clayton saw that. And Pam Grier was in for one day, and she said, 'My hand is better than that.' She pulled up her sleeve, and it's true. Her hand is extremely long and about an inch around at the wrist. It would have worked just as well. It was an embarassment, this hand.\" Jack Clayton himself called it \"appalling\" - but he had an alternative. The sequence would be replaced by an entirely new one in which an army of tarantulas would invade the boys' rooms. Meanwhile Dyer and Clayton and the others tried to concoct a more fitting and comprehensive concept for the Dust Witch. \"She's a very weird creature in the novel,\" Bradbury confessed. I have her coming into the library, and she's got her eyes sewn shut. But we were both afraid that if you didn't do it just right, it was going to be hilarious.\" Clayton took responsibility for cutting the original Dust Witch image. \"I thought it was going too"],["11","Something Wicked This Way Comes","CINEFEX 12 • 11"],["12","Something Wicked This Way Comes","far,\" he commented. \"Now, instead of being awful and very Disneyish, she's beautiful - because that's more exciting and erotic and, above all, tempting, She's there to tempt people.\" Bradbury was happy with the compromise. \"Now she's the Most Beautiful Woman in the World. But occasionally she'll turn suddenly and the kids will see what she is underneath - this ugly, ugly creature.\" Easier said than done. Bob Schiffer, during the original production of the film, had worked long and hard on an image for the Dust Witch, with only limited success. \"We had two or three different changes of concept on Pam. We had her in gold and silver, and in a jeweled face.\" In fact, Schiffer and his associate Stan Winston created over thirty widely variant \"looks\" that the Dust Witch could reveal in her climactic confrontation with the boys. All were put aside.iClayton wanted a face that looked like a walnut shell. No eyes, no nose - nothing. So I gave him a walnut shell - just a thing to fit over the face.\" It didn't work. As the special effects team drew itself deeper into the project, Schiffer's concept drawings were unearthed, and from them a new image was taken - an almost featureless, hairless, frighteningly arid face that would hide beneath the beauty. But there simply wasn't the time or the resources available to recall Pam Grier for a lengthy makeup session - so once again, Lee Dyer's unit was called in. Whole sequences, then, eventually ended up on the cutting room floor: the Tesla generator's lightning bolts, the embarrassing mechanical hand, the successful but unsupported freak tent, and a witch with a walnut face. But the vast majority of the film was retained, and required something that was less drastic but more important - a means, graphic or narrative, to stitch together the disparate story elements that already existed. A creative combination of animation, mattes, and non-animation composites like miniatures and mechanicals seemed to be the answer. \"One thing about special effects people,\" Jesse Silver commented, \"is that they come across a problem they haven't had to deal with before, and they figure out how to make it work. It's all seat-of-the-pants. That's where it's at.\" Dyer, too, saw the goal as imparting a clear continuity through the use of special effects. 'The problem I see in today's movies,\" he said, \"is that you have to be graphic with what you're showing, instead of just implying things.\" One example he cited was the ultimate role of Tom Fury. \"In the original, you have Fury coming to town, and the kids buy a lightning rod from him. They put the lightning rod on the roof, and then you never see it again. There's really no reason for all this to happen.\"There was a similar problem in the climactic scene at the carnival's carousel - a diabolical device that could make a rider grow older or younger with rotations of the merry-go-round itself. In the original cut, sans special effects, Dark was trapped on the magical carousel by a most prosaic means.He got his pant-leg caught in a stirrup,\" Dyer observed. \"And for the Devil to be held down by his pant-leg - I didn't think that was quite real enough.\" To solve the problem, the team created two new characters\" - forces, actually, for Good and Evil. 12 • CINEFEX 12"],["13","Something Wicked This Way Comes","In the case of Evil, the Dust Witch and her people were given \"ectoplasm\" - a greenish, ethereal substance. It would gush from the mysterious train that brings the carnival to town in the opening sequence; it would form into the carnival itself and be sent out by the Dust Witch to do her evil work. \"Now,\" Dyer explained,ithe ectoplasm comes around the chimney where Jim has mounted the lightning rod he bought from Fury. It kind of caresses the lightning rod - which is symbolic of Good - and the rod shoots off a charge of electricity. The ectoplasm backs off very quickly then, so when Will looks out the window, he sees that everything is all right. We give him a reason for that lightning rod.\" In this scene and elsewhere, the ectoplasm is a celanimated effect created through a matte or a burn-in. \"It depended on the surface we were going over,\" Clint Colver recalled. \"We discovered that, with some of these greens, if you get into a low-enough level you can still get away with burn-ins, as long as you're not putting too much light in. If you go up a couple of notches, it begins to look like an obvious animation effect; but when it's kind of dark and dingy in there, it can play pretty The other \"character\" - electricity, the force for Good - was brought in to clear up a number of points as well, and through master manipulation of existing footage made Dark's predicament on the carousel much more serious.Since the good force is lightning,\" Dyer explained, \"we had the lightning hit the carousel. The entire thing was electrified, and Dark was thrown down by it and pinned. You could see his foot clearly in the stirrup, but his body was literally held down by the electricity as he continued to grow older.\" To clearly differentiate between the two \"forces,\" it was decided to make the ectoplasm an eerie, glowing green color with some yellow going through it, as opposed to the blue-white brilliance of the lightning. Both would appear throughout the film, in one form or another, which meant that they would have to keep a fairly consistent look. Maintaining that consistency was one of Dyer's chief concerns. You have to work very carefully with each animator and make sure that all the effects are consistent. With the electricity, especially, it's very easy to get off on different paths.\" A kind of makeshift model sheet for electricity was created. \"Chris Casady, one of the animators, had apparently done some research before,\" Colver said. \"He had a lot of ideas on how to do it and have it look reasonably good. For continuity, we tried to have only one animator work on a single sequence of ectoplasm or electricity - except for some sequences, like the destruction of the carousel, where there are twenty-five to thirty shots involving the lightning. In that case, there was such a bulk of work that we had to have several animators going at once.\" One of the first challenges that Dyer faced was \"creating\" the carnival out of thin air. In the earlier Clayton version, Dark's entourage was just suddenly there, revealed by the rising sun. But the carnival's creation by mysterious and evil forces seemed essential, and an ideal place for the magic to assert itself. For that crucial opening sequence, Dyer turned to Mathematical Applications Group, Inc. (MAGI), and to its creative director, Richard W. Taylor, who had worked with Harrison Ellenshaw as co-supervisor of special effects on Tron. MAGI's computeranimated contribution to Something Wicked is the only significant portion of the film not produced on the Disney lot.iTo do the carnival 'rez-up' with any physical process would have been very limiting,\" said Taylor. \"You just couldn't have gotten the mysterious quality and the camera's flying point-of-view, among other things, by any other method. for this sequence, at least, it was more magic than they could have created in any other way.\" Taylor began by storyboarding the two-minute segment with Lee John Norton. Together they plotted out a sequence in which the camera-eye would travel a twisting and turning path as the circus builds itself from wheeling spiderwebs, malevolent autumnal trees and the ectoplasm of its own dissolving steam engine. Once the storyboards were completed, Taylor and his crew, working both in New York and in California, began generating the primary objects that would be used in the sequence - rough, geometric blueprints taken from MAGI's library, of shapes and from real-life objects in Something Wicked's existing footage. \"These are soft models,\" Taylor explained,ishapes that we could rotate and see from any perspective. Perfecting those shapes took six weeks. Then the choreography began - plotting both camera movements and the movement of the objects in the scene. The goal was to create a \"wire frame\" - a vector animation test that is roughly equivalent to a cel-animated pencil test. Little more than the outline of shapes that would eventually become solid and fully dimensional, the wire test still gave Dyer, Clayton and Disney executives a strong idea of exactly how the final sequence would look. \"While the wire test was underway,\" said Taylor, \"we had other people working on key frames - looking at them as full, shaded pictures, trying to view them as fully realized objects, seeing where the light would come from, trying to make it all look very A special new hybrid system called 'Synthavision\" - a system that combines the complexity of computer animation with the changeable, more organic qualities of cel animation - was also brought into play. Taylor and his team had originally designed Synthavision for John Lassiter's film version of Maurice Sendak's children's classic, Where the Wild Things Are, but Something Wicked would be its first public display. The process began with the high-resolution wire frame test, played out on a 2800-by-4000-line screen. Each frame of that version was blown up and printed on animation bond to serve as an overlay for the Disney cel animators. The sections of the picture that would be hand-animated appeared as empty boxes, but now Disney and MAGI animators produced special images to fill those empty spots - green glowing ectoplasm, waves of mist that peel layers away from the steam engine's wheels and eventually form computer-generated carousel horses, and a shadowy beast-hand that darts out of the wagon-cage straight at camera. Upon completion, the cel-animated footage was shot under a scanner, digitized, and inserted by computer into the high-resolution In a midnight excursion to the freak tent, the boys discover Tom Fury being tortured by the evil Dust Witch. Disney animators generated the electrical discharges after attempts to use a more authentic Tesla coil effect proved unacceptable. / Animated ectoplasm passes between the outstretched hands of the Dust Witch and Mr. Dark. CINEFEX 12 • 13"],["14","Something Wicked This Way Comes","sinister carnival from nearTaylor, creative director for Group, Inc. (MAGI), previews rendering is accomplished coloration, a \"wire frame\" Witch's genuine frightening visage was produced through overlays composited with liveaction stage photography of Computer animation was determined to be the only means of satisfactorily depicting the midnight materialization of Dark's nothingness. Richard W. Mathematical Applications a preliminary vector representation of the sequence. / Before a final with full shading and test is produced to verify proper composition and choreography. / The Dust a series of animation Pam Grier. 14 • CINEFEX 12 shots. The result is a strangely vivid and still naturalistic comBut Taylor did not, and does not, feel that computer-generated animation is incapable of creating organic shapes on its own. There's a common misconception that Tron is the limit of computer animation, which it is not. It was a hard-edged, unnatural-* looking world. We didn't soften it or make it look natural, because it wasn't supposed to be natural. But look at Star Trek Il's 'Genesis Effect.' That was completely organic - and completely computer animation. Hard-edged things have never been the limit of the technology. It's just been the limit of the attempts.\" Synthavision was only part of the process that Taylor employed to give special dimension to his sequence in Something Wicked. He also used two new components - \"paint\" and \" atmosphere\" programs that would make the highly recognizable carnival even more real. The paint program's purpose is self-evident. \"It did what in cel animation would have been done by an ink-andpaint department,\" said Taylor, \"adding smooth, rich color and a feeling of depth to the rounded, soft-edged surfaces.\" The atmosphere program accomplished the same effect for the overall image.The imagery was all naturalistic, as opposed to Tron's hard-edged geometric world. So we put in 'murk' - atmospheric haze that allowed objects to dim in the distance. There's quite a lot of that.\" Sore of the smaller details - gravel on the train bed, wood textures on the planks - were added through the use of photographs of real-life objects. The photos were mapped,\" point by point in the computer, and inserted into portions of the sequence - \"laid on like wallpaper,\" Taylor said, to add yet another layer of reality. The sequence itself ends with one of the stunning combinations of effects that the film uses again and again - a single pullback that begins as computer-generated animation, melts imperceptibly into a miniature shot of the four-foot ferris wheel, and ends as one of Jesse Silver's carefully rendered matte paintings. In the final analysis, it looks like a continuous, smoothly paced and wholly naturalistic reveal of a 1930s carnival, crouched in an Illinois meadow that had been empty only moments before. In creating and executing these illusions, and virtually every other mechanical and optical effect in the film, the effects crew had to strive for a sense of realism and subtlety quite unlike the high-tech, fanciful look of Tron. The watchword was atmosphere. \"Atmosphere is everything,\" Bradbury said. \"You can convince people of anything if you surround them with the proper atmosphere. A real feeling of the weather, the hour, the blowing of the wind. I'll believe anything if you provide me with these touchstones.\" Jack Clayton concurred. \"It's exactly the same principle, basically, as The Innocents. You have to root a fantasy very strongly in reality. That was my ploy as far as this film was concerned.\" The effects creators were equally aware of the challenge - and more. Even the most impossible things in the film had to look more than plausible - they had to look old-fashioned, too. \"If there was a big frayed scratch on a scene in Tron,\" said Harrison Ellenshaw, if somebody'd cha-cha'd over the negative, we'd say: 'Well, that's the way it is inside a computer; you get that kind of negative dirt on things.' But in reality - in 1930 - you can't do that. Everybody knows what a carnival looks like; everyone's been to one. Everybody's registered that in their brain, whether they're an effects expert or a movie buff or not. People in Des Moines will look at it and say, 'Yuck, there's something wrong with that.' They don't know what it is. They just know it's wrong.\" This particular danger, and the attention to detail that the Something Wicked crew applied to circumvent it, is most apparent in the confrontation between Dark and Halloway in the darkened halls of the public library. Dark is doing his best to torment and possibly entice Halloway into joining him, but Halloway is wracked by the memory of his inability to save his son from drowning years ago. Dark offers to make him young again - to tear away the years like pages from a book. And as Dark literally rips pages from one of the library's volumes, each page flares with a glowing orange light. \"When I heard about it,\" said Bradbury, \"I thought, 'They can't do it without being too obvious.'\" And Bradbury was not the only one worried about enhancing this crucial scene. \"I was told that I'd ruin Jonathan Pryce's chance of winning an Oscar if we went ahead and put this special effect in,\" Lee Dyer commented. \"That's crazy. What we were trying to do was add magic to this movie, and this guy Dark was a demon. We had to show he was strong. Done subtly, I thought we could only enhance the sequence - only help it by adding that magic.i Tom Willhite was in favor of the idea. At first, however, Jack Clayton was not. \"It's such a strong scene. It's normally unwise to tamper with a scene of that strength, with distractions.\" So Dyer's unit did only a short sequence - the first three tears of the page - as a test. The response was so enthusiastic that they went on to finish it - with everyone's approval. \"It was done so beautifully,\" Clayton concluded, \"it seemed to be part of the scene.\" A subtle blending of live-action and animation was critical. For his basic approach, Lee Dyer turned first to an animation technique he had employed on a wide range of earlier projects, inперу рук cluding Mary Poppins. \"It was basically a two-pass system. First we did what we called the core pass, which was very hard-edged. Then we went through and animated a diffused soft edge over that - that becomes the color. But here we were dealing with an area of gradation, and it's impossible to rely on diffusion alone to get from a hot to a cool.\" To achieve the desired end, the postproduction crew experimented with an approach grounded in their Tron experience.Normally, what you would do is draw on white paper, then black it in with a Sharpie pen so you'd end up with a solid black area. That's shot with high-contrast film, and then put in the printer. What we've done is broken down an area into three different values of gray - black, medium gray and light gray. Then friskets are cut out of paper for each area, and each area is airbrushed so you end up with one piece of paper with three different gradations. That's shot under the animation crane, on high-con film, and developed in a halftone bath that gives you that gradation. I think it's the first time that"],["15","Something Wicked This Way Comes","CINEFEX 12 • 15"],["16","Something Wicked This Way Comes","16 • CINEFEX 12"],["17","Something Wicked This Way Comes","process has been used.\" The resulting film element, when appropriately colored and run with the live-action, produces a quality of light - as well as interactive light around it - that is so soft-edged and subtle it is difficult to distinguish from the real thing. The animated light can even be made to react to surrounding objects according to their colors or contrasts. \"For instance,\" Colver pointed out, \"in one shot, Dark is wearing a dark jacket, and there are browntoned books behind him. You don't want the same amount of light to come off the black jacket, since it's not a very reflective surface. So a light gray is airbrushed into the appropriate area.\" The dark quality of the original cinematography made mattes unnecessary. \"It's made to order for burning, since it's essentially an additive light situation.\" Another interesting side-point to the library sequence speaks to the heart of the \"Disney style.\" At the end, after Halloway has refused Dark's tempting offer of new youth, the evil owner of the carnival grips the old man's hand and crushes it. To achieve the shot, Bob Schiffer produced a double of Jason Robards' own hand. \"When it was squeezed by Dark, the bone pops through and blood starts to run down.\" The effect was convincing - but worrisome to the Disney executives. They showed it at a preview someplace,\" Schiffer explained, \"and the audience gasped. So they cut it out. I had an argument with one of the heads of the studio... well, a discussion, not an argument - you don't argue with the head of a studio. He said, 'The audience gasped!' And I said: 'What's wrong with that? At least you're getting their attention!'\" What's more, Schiffer pointed out, Halloway goes through the remainder of the film with his hand wrapped in a bloody bandage, and there needed to be a reason for that. \"That's one of the problems at Disney,\" the veteran makeup man sighed. They're still a little reluctant to show blood.\" Dyer and the rest of the crew stood behind Schiffer. \"I thought it was needed,\" Dyer said. \"It really showed you the power of Darki Eventually, the effects people prevailed, and the decidedly unDisneyish scene was restored. Throughout the library sequence, and in virtually every other matte or burn-in effect, the effects crew had to deal with a variety of frustrations.iTron was a first, all the way around, and we had total control over the effects,\" Dyer explained. \"In this picture, we're relying back to the printer, which is your old traditional way of doing effects animation. Under the crane - as with Tron - we could go forty, even sixty or eighty passes if we had to. With the printer, you can go maybe eight, twelve passes before you start to lose. When you're shooting through hi-con film, even though it's a nice, dark black, it's not totally opaque. Some small amount of light will get through, and if you have enough passes downrange, eventually they'll add up to background exposure and start to fog the entire image.\" In two extreme cases, the Disney animators were called upon to use their techniques as a kind of electronic makeup. In one, the Dust Witch turns to confront the boys and her beauty is replaced momentarily by her \"true\" appearance - a hideous, almost featureless visage, derived from one of Bob Schiffer's original concept sketches, that is entirely a product of cel animation. \"The problem was,\" Clint Colver explained, \"we had a shot of this pretty girl we had to uglify. One of our animators, Darrell Rooney, came up with a system. First there's a pass of these weird sort of sewn-together stitches all over her face - in the book, she was a sort of mummy-like presence. Then we added a shady hollowing to her cheeks and the hollows of her eyes in another pass. After we put those two together, we added a faint green tint - and suddenly the pretty face was disgusting. Then the final frosting on the cake - her eyes, on occasion, would pulse.\" The eerie end result was the product of four passes under the animation crane. A similar technique was used to age Robards' reflection in the mirror maze sequence. \"We used a series of mattes, at mildly different percentages, to show wrinkles and depth,\" said Colver, but much more realistically and subtly than with the Dust Witch. I would guess there are about four mattes or so. Two different ones are soft passes for shading-down effects like the shadows under the eyes and the hollowing of the cheeks. One was for putting on wrinkles in the larger areas, and that wasn't quite as soft. Then we used a final pass to highlight the various parts, to show where new light would strike off the wrinkles and so forth.\" In this sequence, accumulated exposure through the mattes was not a major problem, since the animation effects were added by applying shadows \"painted\" on clear film, rather Animation was not the only optical technique used to smooth out and stitch together existing footage. Matte painting, too, played an important part in adding the magic. For this end of the project, Jesse Silver was brought in to work with Michael Lloyd in creating the dozen and a half matte paintings required for the film. Harrison Ellenshaw became involved on a few as well, during Silver's absence due to illness. \"The original film was limited to interiors and very tight shots of the backlot facades,\" Silver said. ¿So primarily, the purpose of the mattes was to open up the picture - to put the town in an environment, and also the carnival.\" Secondarily, story elements introduced in the original version, or through the subsequent use of special effects, could be featured as well. Both purposes are well-illustrated in Michael Lloyd's complex multiplane matte painting of Green Town. In it, the Halloway and Nightshade houses are seen in the foreground, and all of Green Town can be seen in deep background. Between them comes smoke from the steam engine that carries Dark's carnival. All in all, the shot gives the viewer a strong feeling of place. Different elements within the image were painted on four separate glass plates - the foreground houses on one, the train on another, the background village and sky on two more. The completed plates were set up, one behind the other, and then all carefully oriented to a specific camera movement to suggest the illusion of perspective. \"It's a short travel,\" Silver said, referring to the slight, but effective camera move, \"but it's enough.\" Other matte shots were equally effective - the daylight shot of Tom Fury approaching Green Town on foot, another wide establishing shot of the carnival and its surrounding countryside, Jesse Silver adds finishing touches to one of his matte paintings - a view of Green Town nestled in the distance, from the point of view of the carnival site. The painting - photographed with a miniature ferris wheel rotating in the foreground - was ultimately cut from the film / Eighteen paintings did survive, though, including an opening shot of Tom Fury approaching Green Town. Only the road, the field to the right and the first farmhouse down the hill were on the live-action plate. / Recurring storm clouds and the climactic vortex effect were created in Disney's gigantic new cloud tank, employing the now-standard water inversion layer technique. CINEFEX 12 • 17"],["18","Something Wicked This Way Comes","In one dramatic sequence, Cooger climbs aboard Dark's seductive but sinister carousel, and with each backward revolution grows younger until he reverts once again to childhood. In the original cut, the effect was achieved simply by replacing the actor with progressively younger-looking stand-ins as make the sequence more magical, however, Dyer and his team altered the existing footage by \"cascading\" - an optical process whereby each frame is overprinted with others adjacent to it, giving a strobing effect to the action. 18 - CINEFEX 12 Michael Lloyd's rendition of the Halloway rooftop as the ectoplasm invades, and the landscape through which Halloway and the boys run back to the village after the destruction of the carnival. Silver used airbrush techniques, oil paints and acrylics in his. production of everything from storm clouds to landscapes, and he and his colleagues took the extra trouble of working with YCMs - color separation masters - rather than the more prevalent bipack system. YCMs are black-and-white records of the different parts of the print spectrum - yellow, cyan, magenta and black. By using them, we can balance and alter the color in a myriad of subtle ways, so we can achieve the exact kind of balance we want on the plate. With matte paintings, you're always trying to find your optimum balance; and that's one thing you get with YCMs, because we can run 125 frames of slightly varying balances and have them all right there in front of us. It is a little more involved than bipacking. It takes a little more testing and can develop a little more lag time. Another drawback, of course, is that you're losing generations, though we can control contrast when we order the separations made up. On the balance, though, the flexibility we have with YCMs more than makes up for the system's drawbacks. Action that's to be wedded with a painting is done in VistaVision, which gives us pretty much double the usual negative size for a much finer grain structure. At that point, the matte photographer, the artists, everyone gets together, and we pick out what we think is the best shot. We have YCMs made up. We then position the action plate within the planned matte painting by rear projecting the plate on a sheet of translucent material which we adhere to the painting glass. I'll slap on some color, and I'll start doing what we call a 'cinex,' which is basically a color and exposure balancing test. We'll see 125 frames, including several repetitions of the same kinds of balances that grow darker as we go. Then we choose our exposure, as well as color, by ranging the painting. When we find something that looks pretty good, I'll begin to refine the painting. Then we'll run a closer series around that particular balance - what we call a 27-frame cinex. We'll continue to refine the color balance and exposure, and I'll continue to refine the painting, until we feel that we've got a good blend. And if we do it all right, the result is a shot where you can't tell where the matte is. One of the most puzzling aspects of matte painting is the relative indistinct quality of the painting itself.iWhat a shock when I first looked at a matte painting and saw that it was a pile of slop,\" Silver quipped. \"It's carefully planned slop, of course, but it really is quite loose. The trick is to work out where to stay loose and where to add the detail. Remember, you're trying to fool the camera rather than the human eye.\" Still, the contrast between the suggestion of shape on Silver's glass plate and the realistic, detailed look it achieves on screen is remarkable. By using oil rather than acrylic, and by suggesting rather than detailing, the final matte shot loses its artificial, hard-edged quality and takes on a look of startling reality. Cel animation and mattes were aided by a third technology in enhancing Something Wicked's existing footage. For the first time, Disney invested in the construction - under the direction of Ron Tantin - of a cloud tank. Nine feet wide, six feet deep and six feet high, the steel-reinforced plexiglass cubicle holds 2500 gallons of water and weighs 27,000 pounds when filled. \"It's going to have a sixty-hour turnaround between shots,\" Tantin explained during construction. \"We have to drain it and clean it and reestablish the inversion layer.\" The bottom as well as the sides of the tank are optical acrylic - perfectly clear and nondistorting for photography, even though the material is an inchand-a-quarter thick. \"The thing that's unusual about this tank,\" Tantin said, \"is that we have such an area to work with that we can try a lot of things that haven't been tried before. Other cloud tanks are limited by their physical size. With ours, we can theoretically lengthen the take - so rather than have a thirtysecond take, we might stretch it to about a minute-and-a-half, and get a lot more footage, a lot more effects out of it.\" The tank, which took a full month and a half for a crew of six to build and install, includes a sophisticated injection system that can be manipulated from beneath the tank itself by a remotecontrol arm, or \"waldo.\" The tank is calked with a silicone sealer that emits toxic fumes, so the workers inside the tank - the only place to do the sealing properly - had to be rotated on a fairly brisk basis. \"We'll start with standard mixtures of Tempera paint,\" Tantin said, but I've been getting hold of some chemicals to experiment with, and so far the results have been pretty good.\" To make experimentation more practical, Disney has also constructed a smaller test tank. \"The test tank gives us a rough idea of what the final look of the cloud is going to be, and also gives us the opportunity to try different chemicals without spending an awful lot of time and money replenishing the big tank.\" Ultimately, the \"good\" clouds - which emit animated lightning and eventually destroy the entire carnival - were all products of Tantin's new cloud tank. One special effects hookup was achieved using existing footage, without reshooting or employing animation at all - the enhancement of the scene in which Cooger takes a ride on Dark's carousel and grows progressively younger with each revolution. In Clayton's original cut, the actor playing Cooger was simply replaced by a series of younger and younger actors that bore a slight resemblance to him. But the sequence was obviously one that needed magic all its own.iOne of the problems on this film was that they didn't experiment,\" Dyer said, \" and in this case, they gave me the sequence of Cooger filmed only at twenty-four frames per second, which really wasn't fast enough. We tried skip-framing - printing every other frame only - but that was too fast.\" The final solution was achieved through \"cascading\" - printing the images from five consecutive frames all on a single frame, with each image printed at a different percentage. The end product is startlingly similar to the strobe effect achieved in the title sequence to Richard Lester's The Three Musketeers, but in this case was achieved entirely through an optical process. 'There are a lot of things we could have done with the se-"],["19","Something Wicked This Way Comes","quence, given the time and money,\" Dyer added. \"Originally, I wanted to isolate parts of the frame and make them glow; but we had to cut the budget somewhere, and this seemed to be the Clearly, the special effects highpoints in the film's original cut were the masterful makeup creations of Bob Schiffer. An Academy Award winner for his realistic aging of Burt Lancaster in Birdman of Alcatraz, makeup artist on the first color film ever and a renowned authority on beauty makeup as well, Schiffer brought vast experience and enthusiasm to Something Wicked. Schiffer's most impressive sequence is the accelerated aging of Mr. Dark - trapped on his own carousel as he ages a year for every rotation on the evil machine. The actual sequence Clayton captured on film, however, is far different from that which Schiffer envisioned when first involved with the project. Not only did he create the required makeup applications for Jonathan Pryce's face, and design and build a series of puppet heads that could complete Dark's disintegration, he also created a series of storyboards that plotted the entire carousel sequence from beginning to end. It is another \"lost\" section of Something Wicked that, in this case, was never actually filmed at all. I made applications on Jonathan Pryce's face to age and start disintegrating him - but they were very, very difficult. When you have a human face, it's very hard to shrink it; it's always much easier to make it larger. So after the first set of appliances, we sculpted a head - a false head - and the same physical appearance the human had was then transferred to the puppet head. From then on, in four stages, it was a puppet head that was disintegrating - as skeletal as hell. The hands were false, too.\" There was something more Schiffer wanted to try, also. \"I had a mixture of impression material and clay; and I wanted to take a skull and put a heating element in it, and then put this mixture over it, coating the skull and modeling it into a more or less human face. Then, through time-lapse photography, over a time we would see the material bake and crack and the flesh would just peel and fall away slowly. They never did that. The director just wanted them to carry the body away and have a leg fall off.\" Ultimately, Schiffer's storyboards and experimental techniques were never used, and the sequence itself was postponed until principal photography was virtually complete. \"I don't think Clayton ever believed we could make the transference from a human head to a puppet head and not recognize it. The carousel and everything was about to be dismantled when they finally decided to shoot the disintegration scene. I had all these heads lined up - all laid out the way to go - when Clayton saw the heads working, really working for the first time. Then he got excited about the fact that they would work, that they would look like a man. And suddenly he got back into it.\" It seems slightly remarkable, considering the intense postproduction that consumed Disney's finest for so long, that the lion's share of the final film is still Clayton's original footage. Yet, two major sequences were reshot from scratch; and another, brand-new scene was created and cut into the film. One of the reaccomplished scenes takes place near the end of CINEFEX 12 • 19"],["20","Something Wicked This Way Comes","20 - CINEFEX 12"],["21","Something Wicked This Way Comes","the movie, when Halloway is trapped in the carnival's mirror maze and tormented by Mr. Dark. The concept itself had been in from the beginning, but like a number of equally complex and troublesome sequences, was not entirely successful in its execution. \"The whole point of it,\" Dyer said, \"is that Will almost drowned years ago, and was saved by Mr. Nightshade because Halloway can't swim. That's never resolved. It's resolved in a talk between father and son, but it's never really resolved in Halloway's mind. We wanted him to relive this in the mirror maze and resolve it, once and for all.\" The scene had purely visual problems as well. \"The original breaking of the mirrors didn't work at all. The glass they used was safety glass that they set off with charges. It just crumbled.\" There were a number of old men, supposedly living mirror images of Jason Robards grown old, used as well. \"We tried to put it together,\" Dyer continued, \"and became frustrated because we simply couldn't make it work. We didn't plan on bringing Jason Robards back to begin with; but the mirror maze was a big problem, so we had to. We just couldn't go in and hook up footage with existing footage. We thought the best thing to do was redo the entire sequence.i To do it the way it needed to be done meant starting virtually from scratch - a fairly complex proposition that began shortly after Dyer and his crew took up the project. To help suggest appropriate action and camera angles, a four-foot test miniature of the mirror maze was first pieced together. Then the full-size structure - employing thirty-two individual panels of mirrored glass, each almost seven feet tall - was constructed on one of the Disney stages and sheathed in thick black cloth to block out extraneous light. \"We had an entrance of a long corridor, and then a circular-type room,\" Dyer said. \"My original intent was to bring huge shafts of light down through smoke, and you wouldn't know where they emanated from or where they went to. But I was afraid of making it look too much like a disco. So I talked to John Mansbridge (head of the Disney art department) and asked him if he had any oval mirrors in stock. He said he had a couple, but we needed eighteen mirrors in all. So I said, 'Let's take construction board and cut ovals and put them over our own mirrors,' because I think ovals give you that feeling of the Thirties, like Tiffany glass. He cut them for me, and we put them on. It was a perfect motif in that part of the mirror maze.\" In that fragment of the scene, Halloway is directed to look into different mirrors by Mr. Dark's disembodied voice - recorded in early 1983, in a one-day pickup session with Jonathan Pryce. As Halloway looks, the image of Crosetti, and then other villagers trapped in the maze, are superimposed on different mirrors. Then, when Halloway moved deeper into the maze, into another \"room,\" the ovals were removed and the same set was used for a second series of shots.iIt became a totally different room,\" Dyer said. \"So it made the mirror maze look even larger.\" Actual shooting within the maze lasted less than three days, including pickups, but it was one of the film's most difficult sessions. Dry ice was used to create a low, eerie ground fog, but the resultant low temperatures kept technicians and cameramen At the end of the film, Mr. Dark falls victim to his own evil carousel - aging within minutes to a withering skeletal semblance of his former self. Bob Schiffer was responsible for the transformation, starting first with makeup appliances and then progressing through a series of four mechanical heads whose mouths and eyes could be operated remotely. / Jonathan Pryce, in aging makeup, falls beneath the trampling hooves of his carousel horses. / Somewhat older, he struggles vainly to rise. / Animated lightning pins the second of Schiffer's mechanical creations to the floor. / The third, even older, head. / Schiffer's final mechanical artifact clings helplessly to the carousel horse. CINEFEX 12 • 21"],["22","Something Wicked This Way Comes","Means works on one of three constructed for the film. / For several nighttime establishing shots, the twenty-by-twentyfoot carnival miniature was painted backdrop. / To effect which the carnival is sucked mounted upside-down and released sequentially from the inverted platform. Painter Ray Barone provides last-minute touch-up work. / Miniature supervisor Harrison Ellenshaw destruction of the carnival. / On cue, giant wind machines affixed to the platform with Effects technician Robert miniature ferris wheels photographed against a the climactic holocaust in up into the clouds, the miniature set pieces were prepares to signal the begin kicking up a storm, while technicians above release the miniatures, pin releases. 22 CINEFEX 12 bundled up in coats and parkas throughout the day. Camera movement was greatly restricted because of the tricky angles that the mirrors-on-mirrors presented. \"We used fifty-fifty mirror for the camera,\" Dyer recalled, \"but you still couldn't truck. It was all locked down. We did put the camera on tracks at one time, but you could see its reflection in the mirrors.\" What's more, the necessarily tiny cubicle for the camera was barely big enough to hold the operator.iOnly two people could be inside. Clayton had to direct many times without even seeing what was happening. There were times he could be on a ladder looking over the top - in spots where we wouldn't pick up a reflection - but there were several scenes where he would literally have to watch the camera move and call 'action' and hope that he gave the right direction to the actors.\" The breaking of the mirrors posed a problem, too. \"Rather than have the pulverized look of safety glass,\" said Dyer, \"I wanted the glass to come out at you from the left, the right, the back - pieces of it, shattering in shards.i Rather than risk flesh and blood, Dyer called on Bob Schiffer to produce two artificial hands to break through the glass. The final effect was achieved by having the false hands plunge into the glass that covered a pool of water topped with a few inches of mineral oil.iMineral oil is heavier - it makes a better splash. And we overcranked, so it looks very much like glass.\" The shattering of the mirror brings the sequence - and quite nearly the film - to a close. Halloway is tormented by Dark, and eventually shown the vision of his own son, drowning in the river years ago. Dark's words remind him of his painful failure, but this time Halloway does help. He thrusts his hands through the glass, into the water beyond.iHe actually makes contact with the water on the other side, then he goes on further to help Will,\" Dyer explained. iHe pulls him through. He saves his son. So Halloway, as an old man, becomes the hero of the story; and through his attempt to save Will, he in fact breaks the mirror maze. It totally collapses, and at that point Dark becomes aware that the storm has finally come and the whole destruction starts to take place.\" That destruction - in which Dark's evil carnival is swept up in a single twisting column of smoke and lightning - proved to be another major challenge for the special effects team. More than the film's climax, it is a climax of combined special effects, utilizing mattes, cel animation, miniatures, cloud tank effects and all the special sounds that Richard Portman could muster. The task of coordinating the construction, photography and destruction of the carnival miniature fell to Harrison Ellenshaw, cosupervisor of special effects on Tron. 'Miniatures are a bitch,\" Ellenshaw said. Sometimes I wonder why I got into it. They either work beautifully or they don't work at all, and - it's the same with matte shots - if they're five percent off, everybody goes: 'Oh, that's a nice miniature; one of the nicest miniatures I've seen' - and you're dead!' With a highly-regarded background in matte painting, Ellenshaw was a relative newcomer to miniatures. Still, there were similarities between the two disciplines. \"I feel that miniatures do not fail because"],["23","Something Wicked This Way Comes","CINEFEX 12 • 23"],["24","Something Wicked This Way Comes","they re not highly detailed enough. There's always a combination of reasons, but generally it's not the amount of detail.\" Ellenshaw remembered with some discomfort the number of beautiful, detailed models he had seen that were placed in front of bright lights and blotted out. Instead, he takes his cue from Dennis Muren of Industrial Light and Magic, with whom Ellenshaw worked on The Empire Strikes Back. \"Dennis is very good with miniatures. He spends a great deal of time, and sets up each shot very, very carefully. If he has to, he'll spend a week-and-ahalf setting the shot up and doing tests.\" Ron Tantin and Isadore Raponi actually supervised the building of the miniature carnival - which had to match existing footage of the full-size set. We had a little problem finding good reference photos,\" Tantin confessed, \"because there were no dead-on front views of all the facades. We had to compromise with foreshortened pieces, and we did have the working plans from the actual sets. It was a matter of reducing it to the scale we wanted and comparing the picture to the print to match it.\" Even choosing that scale for the four major facades, the tents and various other carnival set-pieces was no easy matter.iYou have to have a certain size for the miniature, Ellenshaw observed, \"but the problem is you don't want to make it too big. Obviously, the bigger it is the more expensive it is, and the more unwieldly.\" Eventually, a one-inch to one-foot scale was settled upon, and the construction work commenced. Ellenshaw had originally hoped to shoot some covering footage outside, but when the full twenty-by-twenty-foot miniature was completed, the complications involved in moving it out were just too much to contemplate, so all the shots were done indoors - which, of course, required a scenic backdrop behind the miniature.iWe had to have a backing big enough to cover everything, because I wanted the sky to be lighter than parts of the miniature. It's true - if you go out at night and look at the mountains and it's a clear night, the mountains are black and the sky is lighter. So you've got to have a backing big enough - which is another reason not to get too large with the miniature. As it was, ours was sixty feet across and went up to the top of the sound stage, about thirty feet. And it's a chunk to light, too. You've got to pump so much light in there to hold focus; and you've got to hold focus in a miniature, or what you get is like the opening to Mister Roger's Neighborhood. Those are the miniatures you don't want to do.\" An extensive photo file gave them most of their clues as to the miniature's final look - with the photographs themselves coming in handy in a different and unexpected way.Some of the facades are photographs,\" said Tantin. ''They're actually set stills that we took the perspective out of by tilting the enlarger. Then we flattened out the prints and pasted them on the facades. It worked really well.\" The paint department came in and touched up any irregularities or inconsistencies. In addition to the carnival tents and attractions, Tantin and crew built three ferris wheels from lead. But unlike the delicate carnival facades, these units were something less than brittle.iWe didn't want them to break,\" Dyer explained. \"I wanted them to be sucked up and twisted rather than shattered. Then we went back and enhanced 24 — CINEFEX 12"],["25","Something Wicked This Way Comes","them with electric arcs and interactive light and so forth.\" The major effect involved the carnival actually being torn from the ground and disappearing into the clouds above. \"The carnival had already been built as a full-scale set,\" Ellenshaw said, \"but because it wasn't exactly practical to suck that up into the clouds, we took the miniature, hung it upside down, and dropped it. We had a big steel framework mounted up in the rafters, and we attached the whole miniature to it. At first, we were going to drop it all in one long shot, against bluescreen. Then we decided we'd do the individual parts first and drop things one at a time, picking angles as we got it up there. We dropped the front facade first, then went back to the second, and used just very quick cuts of all that stuff.\" '¿'Once everything was on the ground, it became a Chinese puzzle,\" Tantin added. \"We took all the parts together, put them right back up again, and went back for a master shot. After the master shot, we attached some of the units to guide-wires to tumble them down through a preset pattern, to try and give it a vortex effect. The clouds were superimposed on that, and all the darkness helped.\" The facades themselves were made from foamboard - a material arrived at only after fairly extensive testing.iYou think just to break a miniature is easy,\" said Izzy Raponi. \"But the fact is it had to go upside-down, and everything had to come apart. We tested with other materials - all kinds of materials to see what was light, how it flew. That's what takes most of the time - to research for the material. By the time you have all the right materials, it takes... well, I don't want to say nothing, but it takes a very short time to build.\" Ellenshaw found himself a little surprised at the power of the effect. Even though we accept gravity as being a downward force, you're surprised when you see it in reverse, as an upward force. If you take that force in our life - that one factor - and turn it upside-down, you become amazed at how powerful a grip it is. It's far more than just a magnetic kind of thing. You see this very easily when you turn something upside down, photograph it with the camera upside-down, and watch it go out of frame at the top. You would never, ever - no matter how hard you worked - get that same feeling by jerking it up with wires. For one thing, you'd be bound to get sections that would drop off. But when everything goes in the same direction, bits and pieces, it really is a good effect. It looks like an unseen power. Since the complete destruction was so complex and risky, Tantin, Ellenshaw and crew began with a series of videotape tests, using white cutouts that were hung upside-down, illuminated in various lighting configurations, and then dropped in winds of varying strength. Before the final destruction took place, the unbroken miniature was photographed for coverage and establishing shots for the opening of the film - for the pull-back that begins with MAGI's computerized animation and ends with a matte shot and the scale model. Then, as the twenty-by-twentyfoot platform, bare of all its buildings, was inverted and hung, each miniature was carefully broken. \"We fragmented all the different units of the carnival into as many pieces as practical, then inverted them over the platform and put a pin release on top. That way, the units could be released from the platform on cue and become a hundred different pieces as they came down.\" The facades were scored, the tents were torn, and each piece was coded appropriately and reassembled - held together with tissue paper and wax. \"It wasn't easy,\" Raponi said, \"and it could easily break whether we wanted it to or not.\" Doweling and balsa wood were used as well - Tantin wanted to keep everything light. \"You get more fragments when the wind catches it. You want a lot of tumbling, and confusion and chaos.\" The destruction sequence as it appears in the film begins with footage of the unbroken model standing right-side up, as a rising wind passes across itiYou see the wind and the tents starting to flap,\" said Ellenshaw, \"and the dust and the dirt come in, and you think that something's going to happen. You think the wind's going to knock it all over, but instead it starts to lift it and...\" Cut to the model hanging twenty feet in the air. Izzy Raponi clambered around on top of the inverted miniature, pulling pins at Ellenshaw's command as three or four different cameras rolled - depending on the take - and large and small fans produced a scaled-down gale force wind that pushed around massive amounts of dirt and debris. Wide-mesh plastic netting in tacked-together frames caught the shattered miniatures as they fell, with the pieces being immediately returned to Tantin's shop for reassembly. Considering the major changes that occurred between the film's penultimate and final versions, it is remarkable that so little new footage had to be produced. In point of fact, only a few actors were recalled at all, and none for more than a few days. And only one major sequence is entirely new - the sequence that came to replace the unsuccessful mechanical hand. \"I got the idea mainly from the public who saw the film,\" said Jack Clayton.There's a scene in the caravan where a spider goes on the boy's hand, and we always got a gasp or a scream. My idea was to replace the mechanical hand with two hundred spiders.\" That alternative was acceptable to Disney executives. Lee Dyer, however, greeted the development with mixed feelings. It was perfect for the film - hundreds of tarantulas invading the boys' bedrooms in the dead of night, crawling on the windows, covering the floor, caving in the ceiling - even nestling beneath the bedcovers. \"I thought if Spielberg could do it with snakes, we could do it with spiders,\" said the effects supervisor. \"Besides, the spider motif is carried on throughout the picture.i But Dyer had a more personal problem to deal with. \"I have a tremendous fear of spiders. It's a real phobia. I can jump ten feet over a little one, let alone a tarantulal But since we were trying to add magic to the film, to add suspense, we had to do it.\" A storyboard artist was called in to elaborate on the sequence. Then, four tarantulas were acquired for study. Cardon Walker, a production assistant on the film, was designated as their guardian. The spiders, in truth, were far less dangerous than everyone had feared. Their bites were not fatal - hardly more serious than a bee sting - and in the weeks they stayed on the Disney lot, no one was seriously bitten. Still, Dyer had to cope with his phobia. The ferris wheel - built from malleable lead - twists and collapses under the gale force storm. / A carnival tent rips loose from the ground. Destruction of the carnival required several timeconsuming takes. First, individual portions of the miniature were isolated and photographed in closeup. Then the set pieces - prescored to break apart convincingly - were reassembled and reinstalled on the platform for a wider master shot. CINEFEX 12 • 25"],["26","Something Wicked This Way Comes","For a nightmarish sequence in the boys' bedrooms, Isadore mechanical tarantulas which connected to a hand-cranked cam mechanism. / The final sequence employed not only Raponi's mechanical spiders, but some two hundred live tarantulas and a like number of rubber artifacts. Outside the bedroom window, spider wranglers attempt to \"direct\" camera inside. / Tarantulas break through the glass and Raponi constructed six could be made to walk by means of invisible wires the squirming creatures without being seen by the swarm into the room. 26 • CINEFEX 12 When we first got them, we had them in an aquarium down in Cardon Walker's office. I was down there one day with a full cup of coffee when one of my animators threw a big rubber spider at me. That coffee lifted right out of the cup. It was incredible Since then, I've found out that maybe fifty percent of the crew had the same fear.\" Eventually, Dyer steeled himself enough to have one of the tarantulas actually placed on his shirtsleeved arm.iThe next Saturday, I had someone put one on my arm without a shirt. It would get up to my bicep, and they would take it off. That was quite a challenge for me.\" And that was as far as he felt he had to go. The shooting itself involved over four hundred spiders - two hundred live ones gathered in Mexico and purchased from Animal Actors of Hollywood, and a like number of rubber models created by the Disney shop from Izzy Raponi's designs. \"One of the biggest problems with the spiders was the danger between themselves,\" said Dyer. \"Although their natural instinct is to run away from their own kind, they would kill one another if left together long enough. We could corral about forty of them together - no morei Another problem was finding a way to herd the spiders without having it show in the shot. Extensive experimentation made it clear - tarantulas could be urged to move most easily by gently blowing air across them with an unheated hair dryer. Subjected to that mild irritant, the spiders would crawl away without bolting. And though they could not climb walls or glass unaided, a sheer film of netting was placed against the set's windowpanes, and they could be held to the glass without discomfort. At one point, a much more aggressive and deadly cousin to the tarantula was imported for some special scenes. \"I wanted to get some real closeups of spiders,\" said Dyer, \"not fighting amongst themselves - because if the spiders represent evil, they wouldn't do that - just being more aggressive.\" The bird spider filled the bill. This vicious spider from South America didn't run from attack - it charged.iThe first time we had it, we put a little piece of doweling in and touched it, and the spider reared back and hit - literally hit that piece of wood. You could hear the fangs striking it.\" The bird spider was shot from a variety of angles in the dim, blue-gray light of the scene's artificial moon, and it - and later, the regular tarantulas - were placed on a manikin shoulder that was dressed in Will's nightshirt, then photographed as if from Will's point of view. One small portion of that take still exists in the final cut. Meanwhile, master modelmaker Izzy Raponi was busily designing and building some remarkable artifacts - lifelike mechanical tarantulas that moved by means of a series of thin, nearly invisible wires running from the individual jointed legs to a box of carefully timed cams. \"We studied a real tarantula for a few minutes a day,\" said Raponi. \"I photographed the tarantula with my mind. We tried to make it as real as possible, because when you're making something mechanical, you're maybe sixty or seventy percent there, normally. If I can give a little more, I try to.\" To achieve as much realism as possible, Raponi and his workers measured the movements of individual legs and the length of a single forward movement. Raponi spent about two weeks making his models. Skeletons were fashioned from wire with joints in each leg, all covered with polyfoam and real hair to give the model an extra-realistic look. Eventually, six fully articulated spiders were constructed, as well as some one hundred and twenty others that didn't move at all, though their wire skeletons and exteriors were similar to their filed brie articulated brethren. Additionally, Raponi built two models whose bodies he filled with a special milky gelatin that would \"squish\" convincingly between the barefoot toes of the boys - cute a made. an effect that made hackles rise on crew members and actors alike. The moving models were virtually indistinguishable from real spiders - not only on camera, but for the spiders themselves. At one point, a wire-guided tarantula was put in with the - point. bird spider, in hopes of producing some vivid and aggressive - and filmable - reaction. 'The bird spider touched the thing,\" Dyer recalled, \"then reared back and struck several times. Then y paused lein it paused, kind of wondering why this thing wasn't dying, why there wasn't any liquid to suck out of it.\" The whole shoot lasted less than a week, complete with the return of Vidal I. Peterson and Shawn Carson - the young actors who played Will Halloway and Jim Nightshade - for some additional footage. With the film's final postproduction sequence completed, the spiders were sold to all comers for ten dollars apiece. Cardon Walker, who had grown rather fond of the creatures during his stint as chief spider wrangler, kept the original utes duriot kids four. School kids bought most of the rest. And in the final analysis, Lee Dyer decided it was all worthwhile.i\"I think you'll walk away from the movie remembering the spider sequence. That's what we're really banking on.\" Computer animation. Matte painting, Cel-animated composites. Physical makeup, mechanical spiders, miniatures, cloud tank effects - virtually every conceivable aspect of special effects technology makes an appearance in a film that, ironically enough, is as much story as special effects, as much subtle atmosphere as flash. The long-awaited screen version of this screenplay-turned-book-turned-screenplay may have taken almost twenty-five years to finally arrive, but everyone from Ray Bradbury to Jack Clayton to Lee Dyer and his crew seem pleased with the results. \"I'm happy with the film,\" Clayton said. \"If I'd had Lee from the very beginning, a lot more of the subtleties would be there now - things that we couldn't put in afterwards. At least it's got characters in it, and a lot of love - unlike a lot of special effects films I could mention, where the people making them didn't seem to think it was necessary to have a story.\" During the final, hectic days of recutting, compositing and looping, Ray Bradbury said: \"I often wonder in the middle of months like this, how it is that any film ever gets made - not just the bad ones, but even the occasional good one. It's a miracle. It's miraculous that a team of forty or fifty people can put up with each other without killing each other, and come out the other end with a thing where they all turn and say, 'Hey, it's our baby.' It's the hardest medium in the world. It looks easy, but it's not.\""],["27","Something Wicked This Way Comes","CINEFEX 12 • 27"],["28","The Howling, The Thing, Krull","STOP-FRAME FEVER ...POST-ANIMATION BLUES"],["29","The Howling, The Thing, Krull","It finally happened, for those who cared - 1981 became a banner year for films featuring stop-motion visual effects. Three major releases flaunting the technique skirted each other by a matter of weeks, and the peculiar fluke in timing made for a mixed bag of illusions and reactions. Ray Harryhausen's Clash of the Titans, offering traditional Dynamation effects and a bluescreenanimated flying horse, snubbed its nose at critical beratement by making one of the biggest financial splashes in M-G-M's history. Dragonslayer took an unexpected bath at the boxoffice and folded its wings, but the research and development team at Industrial Light and Magic made \"go-motion\" a special effects buzz word - eliminating strobe during animation with a rodactuated, computer-programmed \"dragon mover,\" future permutations in the wind. Caveman, the lower-budgeted entry, combined the talents of the best dimensional animators in Hollywood. Under the separate supervisions of Jim Danforth and David Allen, the composites went a beat beyond the standard parameters of Dynamation technique, making the best use of old tricks for comedic effects. Technically conscious audiences scratched their heads in amazement, smiling now and then at Caveman's more recognizable ingenuity. To no one's surprise, the picture came and went, becoming instant fodder for midnight theaters and cable television. None of these respective fates, of course, make any kind of statement on the public's admiration or apathy for stop-motion movies. Good pictures will always shine and survive; foolish ones just fade away - or rise, phoenix-like, as paragons of cult and camp. If the phenomenon of 1981 trumpeted anything, it was a greater appreciation on the part of producers for dimensional animation and its ability to convey the impossible. In partial contrast, 1982 seemed to precipitate a different trend in stop-motion - a trend which, on the surface, reads as discriminating usage in smaller doses for a specific effect, rather than the raison d'etre for a film or mere novelty (Q being the exception). At any rate, old problems and prejudices remain - what's magical for some is terminally unconvincing for others. Intercutting the crisp pseudo-reality of animation with corresponding full-scale mechanical effects invites inevitable matching discrepancies. And unhappily, a tediously realized stop-motion cut is vulnerable to the same editorial quirks as liveaction. There are no balms for ruthlessness when a sequence chugs through a Moviola and a director literally calls the shots at the eleventh hour. David Allen, a diplomatic chap with articulate philosophies on just about everything, offers his own viewpoint. I think one of the problems with stop-motion is that it's something a director perceives, at least subconsciously, as a sacrifice of control. Ultimately his ego takes over and he goes with what he can see. I think that's one of the subliminal reasons Behind-the-scenes still photography by Illyanna Lowry (The Howling), Shinji Nakako (Q), James Aupperle (The Thing) and Keith Hamshere (Krull). Additional photographic contributions by David Allen and Ernest D. Farino. Special thanks to Lili Ungar. why stop-motion has not been used more often. I really don't know how you wrestle with that one.\" Allen has had his share of disappointments. His contributions to director Joe Dante's The Howling were virtually deleted from the film. It was toward the tail end of Caveman, late in 1980, that producer Michael Finnell approached him with a request for a scene of stop-motion werewolves caught in the climactic barn holocaust. The rough cut's montage showed an actor igniting gasoline, rearing back watching it burn, and a slug that said \"Missing Scene.\" Cut back to the actors getting in their car and leaving. Allen was a little uncertain when he left the screening room on Sunset Boulevard. \"I remember feeling that maybe they didn't even need a shot in there. I believe they had shots of hands coming through the cracks in the boards, with greasepaint and hair glued onto them - human hands, basically - and that was it. Then there was this missing scene.\" Earlier on, a complete werewolf suit had been discarded on the grounds that it closely resembled a bear suit, and there would be no way for it to work in the barn where the creatures are seen fully›transformed. Rob Bottin, in charge of special makeup effects, had been wrestling with the problem, but most of his energies were channeled into the shape-shifting area. Consequently, Bottin's associate Jeff Shank embarked on an alternate idea to be used for closeups in the barn - a seven-foot-tall rod puppet controlled from behind by levers. Dave Allen was hired two months later, and the idea then was to intercut his animation with the live-action rod puppet. Translating the anatomy of this monstrosity to a functional animation model was a grueling thought - and hardly feasible. \"It had a pinched waist like a greyhound,\" Allen recalled, with a full chest and almost no shoulders. That part was nice and dog-like, but I felt the legs were too thin and overly triplejointed. I believe they built it that way so they could fulcrum it up in the air, making it appear to rise to a threatening height. It all seemed too precarious to me. I knew it would never look good to have models walking around on legs like that. Since they said that the legs would hardly ever show, they allowed me to change them to my own liking. In fact, they felt that they'd be using less of the rod puppet - that they wouldn't object to any differences in my models. In the end, they didn't use the rod puppet at alll I remember going to the stages toward the end of the film and seeing it hanging out in a corner.\" Roger Dicken sculpted the animation models, working from Dave Allen's comments and design of the armature, which Allen did with Ernie Farino. Interestingly, the armature came from an assemblage of joints that had originally been built for the Davey and Goliath TV series. Farino worked them into a new arrangement while Allen planned his animation setups with Dennis Gordon, an expert woodcarver who had previously built the large arena miniature for Allen's ill-fated The Primevals. For the Howling, Gordon constructed the miniature barn, along with tiny props and related rigging. And in order to stage the frenetic panarticle by tomime properly, Allen filmed his associate Randy Cook going Paul Mandell through the motions (Cook likewise filmed Allen gesticulating)"],["30","The Howling, The Thing, Krull","For The Howling, David Allen prepares Roger Dicken's clay Farino, Allen fabricated three stop-motion puppets which were used to produce four quick cuts for the film. / Two of the cuts depicted the fully miniature by Dennis Gordon. / During animation, flame and smoke footage was rearprojected behind the barn set so that minute flashes could be seen through cracks in the timber. Interior flames were added through a separate pass on just the foreground beams and miniature props, behind. / An exterior shot of the werewolves on a fogstop-motion scene to survive the final cut, and it was used prototype werewolf for casting. Working over armatures by Ernest D. transformed werewolves trapped inside a burning barn, constructed in with the process screen bound road was the only merely as part of a transitional dissolve. 30 - CINEFEX 12 and studied the action frame by frame on a converted Super-8 projector. \"I'd never done that before and had always wanted to,\" admitted Allen. \"I found that working from live-action as a guide has drawbacks - but it definitely has its advantages.\" Using miniature sets and process projection, Allen devised four animation cuts. Cut One had flames licking across the floor with the werewolves looking down at them. Cut Two had the flames creeping upward, with one wolf choking, gasping for breath and finally collapsing, and another one beating on the door. Both shots employed the same miniature barn and same camera angle. Cut Three had three of the wolves standing in the fog, surveying the getaway car. Cut Four started on a scene of actress Dee Wallace running down a hill in semishock and pausing by a tree for a beat, the camera trucking with her. As she stopped, the camera was locked off, enabling Allen to dissolve in a splitscreen and animate a werewolf peeking out from behind the tree, menacingly. Clever as it was, the shot was doomed. \"It was just an atmospheric thing, really,\" Allen shrugged. \"Apparently they found no place for it in the picture - it didn't seem appropriate to them to have the wolf pop out at that moment. When I learned it had been cut, I suggested that they could certainly make use of it after she exits; it then becomes a separate shot of just the woods and the werewolf stepping into view.\" Mike Finnell admitted the idea had merit, but since Allen's suggestion came only a day before the film was to be scored, the final cut had already been locked in and the scene was not included. The first two cuts required some in-camera ingenuity. Out in a parking lot, the regular special effects crew engineered flame and smoke, which Dave Allen photographed himself. The flame element was rear-projected behind the miniature barn during animation, visible only through cracks and holes in the wood. Foreground fire was another matter. Gross superimposition of flame over the animation would have resulted in a \"pasted on\" look. To avoid that, Allen had Dennis Gordon build the foreground beams and flour sacks on a separate level. Animation completed, the film was wound back in the camera and the barn set was removed, leaving only foreground beams and the process screen. Then Allen rear-projected an appropriate flame plate, keeping the foreground structure in silhouette. The holdout allowed him to insert licks of flame \" around\" the beams on a separate pass. On a third pass, the smoke element was projected up and superimposed.iUsing your Mitchell like that can really cut down on your optical bill,\" Allen pointed out. \"It was all done on one piece of film running back and forth in the camera. The smoke did a good job in taking the edge off the miniature look.\" Ordinarily, such a multiple pass would require precise exposure wedges - one bad superimposition and your stop-motion is ruined. Allen felt confident enough to do it all without tests, judging relative exposure values by eye. The shot of the werewolves on the foggy road combined stopmotion with a rear-projected clearing, shot in Griffith Park. Fog machines rolled mist across the trees, adding atmosphere to the plate. Allen photographed a separate fog element and doubleexposed it over his animation, again by backing up the film in the camera and shooting off the same process screen. Lamentably, a mere smidgen of this one shot - superimposed over a slow dissolve to the city - was all that remained of Dave Allen's scenes for The Howling. Only a day before the film was being scored did he hear of the grim news. Shortly after I was hired, the picture was refinanced and it went back into production. At that point, Joe Dante and Mike Finnell surveyed what they had and felt it needed heavier production value. Unfortunately for the animation, many of the changes they made went deep enough to affect what I had been doing. Apparently they did a lot of work on the barn burning, Scenes of the actors being threatened by the flames were either shot over or salvaged from before and cut into the new version. As a result, when they cut inside the barn for the first time, it's already like an infernol Everything is immediately orange with fire. That didn't work with my whole approach to the puppets. I had created two little cuts in there, but by the time those cuts were ready to be delivered, they had a whole different montage worked out - and my cuts just didn't splice in anymore. The feeling was different when the picture resumed. They may have had the perception that stop-motion had a different look. Naturally the animation would have worked better had it been designed more consistently with the picture, rather than just paying me to shoot some scenes and plugging them in without any coordination of production design. Throughout the film, you were seeing any number of techniques. Animation, of course, was one more point of departure in getting everything to look unitized. But that was hardly the problem. You just can't go very well from scenes of John Carradine hissing through werewolf fangs to the shots that I did. Perhaps the lines of communication weren't clear enough. For instance, they later built another suit and used it extensively, unbeknownst to me, and that suit corresponded less to my stop-motion puppet than the rod puppet did I think that when The Howling went into its second phase, they kind of half-forgot what I was doing. In the meantime, I was still grinding away. So there you are.\" At the mere mention of Q, Randall William Cook starts gnawing on his pipe stem.It's a low-life, cheap-jack monster movie - and boy does it look it! The whole thing, my involvement included is an aesthetic crime. I throw myself on the mercy of the court.\" Cook, who designed the pseudo-mythological beast and co-animated it with Dave Allen, understandably finds objectivity rather untenable. Originally penned as Q: The Ultimate Terror!, then changed to The Winged Serpent and finally Q, the film was producer-director Larry Cohen's attempt to unleash upon modern audiences the deja vu of such Fifties delights as Rodan and The Giant Claw, and turn a quick profit on a limited run. The situation was nothing new for Allen and Cook, who since 1976 have made a career of supplying abysmal productions, such as Laserblast and The Crater Lake Monster, with superior stopmotion sequences, thereby providing them with their only viable selling points. Q fell neatly into the same category. And unlike The Howling, Cohen needed every foot of animation Cook"],["31","The Howling, The Thing, Krull","CINEFEX 12 • 31"],["32","The Howling, The Thing, Krull","32 - CINEFEX 12"],["33","The Howling, The Thing, Krull","and Allen could devise. It became apparent that Larry's thinking swung quite radically from the time we were first hired,\" Allen recounted. \"At first, he just wanted a few wing flaps, out of focus, to be printed over his New York helicopter footage. Believe it or not, I think he was hoping to get away with implying almost everything that needed to be explicit, simply by using POV shots. In fact, he was going to use an optical effect, which he tried with Howard Anderson - a red-green effect of what the creature sees with its limited color vision. He tried some tests which looked okay, but he decided that it was too derivative of the effect that was done for The Wolfen and junked it. So at the start, it was just a wing beat here and a talon there. But after seeing some of our scenes, it was: 'Hey, we've got a creature that looks pretty good. We could choreograph specific things for it to do. And maybe we needn't be so shy about showing him off.' Frankly, I don't know what Larry would have done had he not found Randy and me. It certainly would not have been quite the same picture.\" Cook was trying to get his own film, Graveyard Shift, off the ground when he received a call from makeup artist Steve Neill. Apparently Cohen had hired a New York propmaker to do a big serpent head, along with the full-scale taloned feet that were used on location. Somewhat disenchanted with the head, he then hired Neill to do another mockup. Eventually the big head idea was axed completely, throwing everything back into the design stage. At that point, Cohen began to seriously explore the stopmotion route. Steve Neill referred him to Cook, who did a pencil sketch of a serpent. Cohen compared it to another rendering Jim Danforth had done and, after weighing all the factors, decided to go with Cook. \"Larry wanted to see a complete design breakdown of the thing. I was a bit suspicious of him, since all we had talked about were these amorphous shapes zipping by the nothingstar the camera. I drew up a flying dragon which looks nothing at all like Quetzalcoatl, but I thought that a naturalistic, sculptural rendering that was faithful to Aztec mythology would be too boring for my taste. Besides, I had a dragon design that I'd wanted to do for ages. So I dredged up this old chestnut, and Larry bit on it.\" Cook phoned Dave Allen during his negotiations with Larry Cohen, and the craft end of the project began at Allen's Burbank studio shortly thereafter - on a very sparse budget. Literally all it was costing Larry was our basic studio overhead, Randy and me on salary for that period of time, and Dennis Gordon making miniatures on the side.\" While Cook sculpted the prototype, Allen began on the armature, the body of which combined parts of the Howling werewolves and some disparate pieces left over from Caveman. The wings were actually tooled by Marcel Delgado for a dimorphodon in Creation, circa 1931 - workable relics Allen had acquired after the death of animator Pete Peterson. Allen first used them for his harpy in the unreleased Witches' Brew, stripped them for the pterodactyl in Caveman, and reworked them again for Q. When the assembly was completed, Randy Cook sculpted directly over it and made the mold. Then Allen, who was only technically involved with the model, cast the rubber. With wings outstretched, the serpent measured slightly under three feet. \"Going in,\" stated Allen, \"I'd like to be on record as saying that the work on Q was mostly good fun. Larry was very flexible with us, and we had a great deal of freedom within the obvious limitations. Of course, since we were presented with a 'finished' film, there were no storyboards at all to begin with. Randy boarded a few things and Larry photographed new scenes to satisfy certain concepts - pickup shots in New York of people gaping in awe and new plates of the Banker's Trust building for the shot of the bird crashing into the tower.\" Alterations were also introduced in the sequence filmed high atop the Chrysler Building - most notably in the shot of David Carradine bolting in surprise at the serpent's sudden appearance, and a follow-on of him and a buddy charging up to the opening, with the bird flying away in the distance. For the first shot, Cohen had originally planned to use the big mockup head - but apparently it looked terribly artificial. With stop-motion animators at his disposal, the scenes could be realized more credibly. Ultimately, Cohen decided on a makeshift bluescreen composite, erecting a painted blue muslin in his driveway and lighting his actors against it at night. Later, Randy Cook accomplished the animation elements using a separate foot-long serpent head, armatured from the Caveman tyrannosaurus skull and other tail and neck pieces. Eventually, the shots were composited at Van der Veer Photo Effects. As tacky as the setup sounded, the final composites were virtually By and large, the biggest problem was securing rear projection plates for animation. That Allen was able to find usable material was a miracle in itself. \"Nothing was steady. I remember sifting through hundreds of feet of helicopter footage, trying to find parts that were stable enough to go behind a creature without a lot of background 'ride.'\" \"Larry wouldn't let us reshoot,' added Cook. \"Everything was shot from the creature's POV, leading one to conclude that Larry thought he could get away without showing the thing at all! Since we couldn't go back to New York and photograph the stuff correctly, Dave and I lied to ourselves that we could make these pathetic 'copter shots work. Out of miles of footage, we picked a two-second chunk here and a .8 second shot there. The extensive effects at the end were largely done as afterthought.\" Somewhat eccentric was Dave Allen's principal stop-motion flying technique. Generally, there are three ways to go - animating the model on a pylon against a blue screen, rigging it on piano wire in front of a process screen, or mounting the model on a sheet of plexiglass for alternate frontlight-backlight exposures. For Q, Allen devised a simple mixture of the latter two methods. At almost all times in the film, the serpent was mounted to a piece of glass, which was encased in a moving compound in front of a process screen. The glass could move left-toright and up-and-down in front of the projected background, and be animated accordingly. Affixing the puppet to the glass was a rod, 3⁄8-of-an-inch wide, with a Universal hinge on its end that hooked into the bird's body. The rod was secured to the glass by nuts and washers on the reverse side. The system avoids matDavid Allen and Randall William Cook provided afterthe-fact animation footage for Q inserting a giant flying serpent into numerous moving background plates photographed from a helicopter over New York. / To create motion blur, and thus avoid strobing, doubleexposures were often employed - particularly on the downflap when the creature's wings were passing quickly through the frame. I A helicopter plate is processprojected behind the rigidly mounted puppet as the serpent makes a strafing run on the Chrysler Building. / Allen fashioned the Quetzalcoatl armature from a collection of existing joints and pieces - some predating the original King Kong. CINEFEX 12 • 33"],["34","The Howling, The Thing, Krull","A twilight shot of the beast flying over the city. For this, and numerous other flying mounted - on its cameraaway side - to a glass plate which could be moved left-toright and up-and-down to afford the puppet with several axes of movement against the stationary process screen. / Though most of the animation achieved through traditional techniques, some - including this street level up-angle - were combined optically, with the serpent and the miniature pyramid-rooftop to which it is clinging matted into the livescenes, the puppet was composites for Q were rear-screen process action plate. 34 - CINEFEX 12 ting costs and unstable aerial rigs,\" Allen explained, \"but the bird can't really turn and expose both sides in the same scene. He has to remain more or less in the same attitude throughout the shot. However, since the puppet has a number of entrance points for mounting, the angle can be varied, and a certain amount of pitch and yaw can be attained. If you design the shots correctly, there's no reason for you to expect to see both sides, anyway. Of course, there are ways of getting rid of the rods. In a bigger budget picture, you can roto them out. But then, of course, you wouldn't be working in front of a process screen.\" Allen and Cook were able to achieve reasonable blurs with this rig by moving the drive screws during the actual exposure. Blurs were a virtual necessity in Q. Without them, any animation model constantly flying by the camera would strobe terribly, especially in tight shots. Considering that the blurs were largely done by hand, without the aid of a motion control camera, makes their effectiveness all the more appreciable.iThere are several shots that I feel are good examples of the system working at its best,\" Allen pointed out. \"One is of the monster when it has just plucked a bather from a rooftop pool and is flying away. It s the first time you see it in full, and you're getting a blur that's just about perfect for the amount of gain on the frame. Then Randy did one - a tight closeup of the body going by, like a shark swimming past a porthole in an aquarium. And there was another where the monster flips a workman in the air. With mattes, you couldn't get those kinds of edge characteristics, because it's a true blur in front of a background. Coordinating the move of the model with the cycle of the camera shutter, though, is sometimes something you just have to feel. It's like grandma's biscuits - she can't tell you how she made 'em; she just knows the feel of the dough.\" There was also another item that greatly facilitated Allen's blur program - an incremental timing device governing the camera's shutter.We used electronic timers for exposures, as we had on Caveman,\" Allen disclosed. \"Jim Danforth designed them, and we discovered that they were quite accurate. In fact, they were so constant, so consistent, you could build up exposures. For instance, if you had a four-second exposure on a frame, you could make up four one-second settings on the timer for that frame to 'spread' the action across the blur. And you would not get an exposure difference in that area. It's surprising, really, because you'd at least expect a reciprocity difference - but they were perfect! Then, when you didn't need the blur anymore, you'd go back to the four-second exposures and carry on as usual. We did that quite a lot on Q. I don't recall us doing a full four exposures on a frame, but often we'd shoot it twice. Of course, you'd never want to do the whole movie that way, but it was very effective on the wing flaps. Randy and I double-exposed many frames on the downflap. Your eye doesn't see that being done, but it helps subconsciously. It's part of why the bird looks nice and heavy.\" Unhappily, no system is free of unwanted concomitants, and in the case of the rod-mount arrangement, glass reflections became a precarious affair. One shot markedly suffered, requiring a fast cutaway. It was a case where Allen had to match the lighting of the puppet to that of the projected buildings, which were all backlit and mostly in shadow. To do so, he mounted a light above the process screen to backlight the creature. What that did, predictably enough, was light up its off-camera side. \"I actually saw it through the camera while I was shooting it,\" Allen admitted, \"but I was into it by then. Sometimes it's hard to know what the film is going to see; but as it turned out, it was over the edge. It really isn't kosher, and on another movie you might have done it over with some other system or scrapped the shot. But Larry needed anything he could get.\" Several scenes were done on wires in order to achieve a move too cumbersome for the rod mount. One was the \"pyramid shot\" animated by Randy Cook, where the wounded serpent smacks into a tower. The tower itself was a partial miniature matted onto the plate. Cook decided to embellish the shot by having a piece of debris fall off the building and go \"through\" the matte line, which he achieved by filming the animated chunk on both sides of the split screen. Another wire rig was used by Dave Allen for the bluescreen tower composite of the serpent flying away in the distance. Allen and Cook also wire-animated several people falling from miniature baskets on the Chrysler Building. \"Those were process shots photographed with a 40mm lens,\" said Allen, i\"and I would say they were moving about five feet. I set up a wire harness on a one-by-two and moved it on a track - one of several such rigs I've used over the years. The models were swung back and forth during the exposure for blur. You have to be very careful, because the wires can end up bobbing all over the place. That required a four-second exposure, because the process image was quite large and the light was more dispersed. The background for that scene was actually a single frame of workprint shot from one of the helicopters. We had no other way of getting a locked-off scene. In fact, the frame we used was taken from the scene of Michael Moriarty looking down and realizing how high he is. There's a little scene there with a quick pan, and it's from the same roll number. I almost threw the idea out; it sounded too far-fetched. But Randy was intrigued with the possibility of making it work, and he talked me back into it.\" \"We thought it would be nice to have someone falling through the air,\" Cook elaborated. \"I built this little SWAT man for one shot: you saw him several times as it turned out. I thought, 'Great, but we don't have a plate looking down on the street.' Dave was walking across the studio and stepped on this piece of film lying on the floor. And one frame from that scrap was used as the platel Almost all the static process plates were done that way, although sometimes we'd find a marginally acceptable frame and print it over and over again. Of course, it looked like hell.\" Scavenger work notwithstanding, the greatest source of heartburn for Allen was the frontlight-backlight matte work, which was divided between two optical houses. For reasons that were never totally clarified, many of the elements appeared to be contaminated, resulting in bleeding matte lines and poor densities. Allen was stymied.iI always use Dick Bond over at Master Film"],["35","The Howling, The Thing, Krull","Effects for front- and backlight shots. But when it was determined that Larry Cohen wanted a shot of the bird flying behind the Chrysler Building, I introduced him to Pete Kuran, who did a very good roto on the side of the building once we found a section that was steady enough. Not surprisingly, Pete likes to remain in touch with the whole job after he's completed his part of it. He has a separate optical company that he uses, and he felt he could put the whole shot together, rather than deliver elements to Dick Bond. Because Pete's work is always so immaculate, I trusted his sense of taste. The wedges looked mostly okay, but to my astonishment, the shots looked totally different. There were all kinds of optical problems. Apparently the people who processed them were attempting to be creative and they elected to do tonal mattes instead of just working from hi-con. However, that raised problems they were never quite able to solve. Larry Cohen approved it, though - so I was kind of caught. But the one shot that Dick did of the serpent hanging onto the building with the people looking up was perfect. It was unfortunate that he didn't do them all.\" Pete Kuran nonetheless did some inventive work for Q, including a cel-animated shadow of the serpent strafing the Manhattan skyscrapers, which is entirely convincing. He also incorporated animated rifle fire effects into the SWAT sequence, giving rather dead scenes some added life. Randy Cook spiced up a few of his own scenes by reflecting gunshots into the process work with a two-way mirror arrangement, introducing it into the photography rather than going to opticals and sacrificing another generation. Dennis Gordon constructed several impressive miniatures, including the gutted attic nesting spot for the end scene - built on a four-by-eight foot table - and a threefoot miniature of the Chrysler Building tower for the bluescreened animation. For the scene of the men hanging from their work baskets, Gordon created a forced perspective model of the Chrysler Building, thereby eliminating the problems of carrying focus and accidental collision with the process screen. In Allen's opinion, the most successful scene in the picture is that of the baby serpent emerging from the egg. The grotesque fledgling was actually a hand puppet built by Steve Neill, shot on two separate nights. Dennis Gordon fabricated the two-foot egg, Randy Cook puppeteered the creature from beneath a table, and Dave Allen shot it from the loft of his studio ceiling.iWe actually did one stop-motion shot with the hand puppet,\" Allen revealed. \"It's the only composite shot where you see the baby bird and the people in the same scene. That was done by advancing the projection and having Randy move his hand a bit. We pixilated it and it worked fine. A large bird shell had been built for the liveaction, but we just eclipsed it with our model.\" The all-too-familiar cutting room scenario that sealed the fate of the stop-motion scenes for The Howling reared its head once again during the wrap session of The Thing, much to the chagrin of animation supervisor Randall William Cook and his small cadre of technicians. The work itself was an eleventh hour deal encompassing miniature set construction, sculpture, intricate CINEFEX 12 • 35"],["36","The Howling, The Thing, Krull","36 - CINEFEX 12"],["37","The Howling, The Thing, Krull","armature design and animation, all of which had to be finished within the incredibly short span of three-and-a-half months. In essence, The Thing was a garish showcase for Rob Bottin's effects makeup work - the culmination of every special mechanical effect he'd ever developed in an effort to fulfill director John Carpenter's quest for the ultimate monster. Cook was no stranger to Bottin, having worked with him on Tanya's Island (\"1 painted the ape's face and hands') and, ironically enough, on The Howling, for which he designed \"the cutsie-poo werewolf at the end, which looked like Jean Marais in drag.\" Rob Bottin and John Carpenter claimed that the work on The Thing had been so extreme and overwhelming that the only way they could come up with a topper would be to have it grow to enormous size for a climactic impact. The only feasible way to render it was in stop-motion. \"They wanted lots and lots of cuts,\" said Cook, wincing at the memory of the time squeeze. Fortunately we were able to talk them down to five. We didn't start until the end of January 1982, and we had to finish by the Scratching of May. And everything had to be started from A scale was determined after Cook figured out what the creature - dubbed by the filmmakers as the Blairmonster - was go ing to look like. \"Initially they had a concept that had been de vised by Rob Bottin and conceptual artists Mike Ploog and Mentor Huebner, which looked like a man on top of a tentacle, with another bunch of tentacles emerging, Rob and I added more elements to it, kicking around suggestions. The conversation went: 'Why don't we put a mouth on the side of the head?' And I'd say: 'Yeah, let's make it a bug mouth.' Once that was figured out, I sculpted and built it, with an assist from animator Carl Surges on the body and tentacles.\" The Blairmonster, in its final state of grotesquerie, transcended the conventional requirements of stop-motion craftsmanship. A number of structural problems had to be surmounted. For one, a dog was supposed to rip through the Blairmonster's stomach, making it imperative that the joints in the ankles and feet for that section be small enough to conform to authentic canine anatomy. The actual burst was to be engineered by the full-scale \"A team\" on a stage at the Universal-Hartland effects facility, and then picked up by Cook's animation as the dog sailed through the air on its umbilical cord. In addition, four tentacles were conceived as constantly undulating, along with a human arm and two writhing \"bug arms\" emerging from the torso and shoulder areas, making for rather complicated armature work. Having worked with him on Caveman, Cook hired Ernest D. Farino to tool the intricate metal skeletons. Jim Danforth had designed the Caveman armatures and had most of the pieces made; Farino's job was to assemble them and make new pieces for the horned lizard. That experience proved advantageous here, as Farino was able to acquire some tubular collar joints that had been machined for Caveman but not used. Then it was a matter of taking the designs conceived by Cook, Bottin and Carpenter and translating them into work drawings and blueprints. Farino did the armature work at Universal-Hartland, taking advantage of their opulent machine shop, complete with milling cutters and lathes. Cook's sculpture began with the big worm-like tentacle that served as the Blairmonster's understructure. He and Carl Surges fashioned the clay over a rough assemblage of joints, while Farino started on the Blairmonster armature. When that was done, Cook sculpted directly over it while Farino began tooling the tentacle armature. Everything was coordinated simultaneously. \"It was an interesting situation,\" said Farino. \"The Blairmonster had two insect arms and a human arm - all at different shoulder levels - and a gaping jaw on the side of his head. Originally, I went through the trouble of putting in upper and lower fangs on those jaws, which were connected to tiny ball bearings in sockets. The mandibles could be animated, but I guess Carpenter felt that wasn't a good idea, as it was dropped. The armature was particularly difficult because it had to be designed in three dimensions. If you have a human puppet or a gorilla, you can design it fairly well from the front view, because you have all the proportions you need. But this one was different. Randy was able to come up with front and side-view drawings, which made my job easier. I did tracings over it and did the mechanical drawings to fit the dimensions of the sketch. The size. was comfortable from Randy's point of view. Sometimes when you get too small, it's hard to make joints that keep a usable tension. Yet you don't want to get too big because the overall weight of the puppet is increased, making it more difficult to work Creating a stop-motion Blairmonster was a tedious affair. The midpoint of the torso contained a body block and a hole from which the umbilical cord could emerge. Initially, Farino misunderstood the mechanical concept of the cord - presuming from the storyboards that the dog would simply lurch out, rather than drop down and sway - and simply connected the dog and torso with a rod. Cook informed him otherwise, and Farino set about articulating it with a whole series of collar joints. The understructure of the large support tentacle was problematic in that it had to hold its own weight. Anchoring it on either side like a suspension bridge naturally invited sagging. Farino had to We were able to acquire some old joints - heavy sandwich plates with ball bearings between them - that Steve Czerkas had made a long time ago. I used five or six of them on the big tentacle, which I knew would not require any delicate undulation. I reworked some of the sockets; they were strong enough to sustain the weight. Then Jim Aupperle brought in these two heavy sculptor's pedestals that could be cranked up. The armature was designed to be bolted down on the pedestals with mounting plates which were underneath the set.\" The dog miniature was a bit more intricate. Here, Randy Cook was faced with having to sculpt the model first. The armature was then built to fit the mold - a generally less-desirable technique since the mold can be damaged by the constant insertion and removal of metal. A very conscious effort was made to make the legs as thin as possible, avoiding the bottom-heavy limbs A larger-scale head and neck section was constructed for some of the closeups. Though many of the machinegun flashes incorporated into the climactic battle sequence were added optically, Randy Cook inserted some directly into his closeups by rigging a beam-splitter in front of the camera and reflecting in single-frame bursts of light. / The serpent grabs a stopmotion SWAT man from a work basket. / A flailing man plummets to his death. The stop-motion puppet, animated by Dave Allen, was suspended from wires in front of a process screen and swung back-and-forth slightly during each four-second exposure to create a naturalistic blur. / Cook, who designed and sculpted the winged serpent, paints a small section of latex skin into the head and neck mold. CINEFEX 12 • 37"],["38","The Howling, The Thing, Krull","Randall William Cook adds detail and color to the halfman, half-insect Blairmonster torso designed for The Thing. / The base upon which the wormlike body with smaller tentacles extending from it. / Ernie Farino's armature for the tentacled understructure. / Constructing the head and particularly taxing - not only complexity, but also because it violated nature's basic rules of symmetry. The dog, made to burst from the creature's armature. / The Blairmonster - placed in Susan Turner's miniature replica of the ice cave set - was photographed stage photography. The final effect was considerably more low-key and atmospheric than Stop-motion supervisor torso rested was a large torso armature was because of its inherent stomach, required yet another fully-realized by cameraman James Aupperle, who strove to duplicate the lighting employed in the full-size shown here. 38 • CINEFEX 12 that occasionally plague animation puppets. Six weeks of miniature set construction transpired, the work of Susan Turner and assistant James Belohovek. Well-versed in both cel and stop-motion animation, Turner was already on the project when Randy Cook was hired, having previously designed and constructed the film's saucer-like spaceship under the aegis of Peter Kuran at Visual Concept Engineering. \"They gave me a portion of an existing full-size set that I had to duplicate,\" Turner explained, \"and they wanted it to be totally realistic. I did it on a two-and-a-half-inch scale, at Randy's request. Taking a four-by-eight foot piece of flat foam, I started digging into it, creating the basic shape of the miniature set. It was very difficult trying to copy something like an ice cave. I set it up on a four-byeight foot plywood framework so Randy would be comfortable standing in front of it during his animation. Fortunately, I had a lot of cooperation from the art director, John Lloyd, who described the functional aspects of the full-scale set. That helped me a great deal.\" Plaster was used for a stable animation surface, and props - including tiny Molotov cocktails - were baked from sculpey and casting resin. Dozens of barrels were fashioned from cardboard tubing, and special attention was given to a miniature lantern, deciding what color it should be and whether it would cast a natural shadow or use a painted one. \"I had to build the entire set before anyone knew exactly where the hole would be for the tentacles to come through,\" Turner continued, \"and since I already had all the miniature barrels in place, I had to be very careful that everything didn't collapse when I was cutting the For his cameraman, Cook chose James Aupperle, whose prior experience with lighting stop-motion setups in such films as Planet of the Dinosaurs and Caveman made his hiring essential. At first, Aupperle spent most of his time devising an overall lighting scheme for the entire set - one that would work almost everywhere, as Cook hadn't yet decided on his shots. Once that was done and the camera angles were locked in, Aupperle's major concern was creating a miniature representation of flickering firelight, which had to match perfectly the pattern and intensity of the live-action production footage.iI'd study trims and outs from the sequence and basically figure out the highs and lows of it. There were several tests just to get the flickering down, and a few more to give it some atmospheric perspective. They had used a lot of smoke in the live-action, which tended to grey out the shadows. To match that, I had to find the right combination of low contrast filters. We didn't shoot any smoke into the miniature set, though. We thought about it, but it would've taken a lot of time to build a smoke-filled environment on one of Hartland's larger stages.\" Aupperle discovered that the firelight effect on the live-action photography was achieved by varying the voltage on a powerstat arrangement, dialing the light up and down. To get the same effect in miniature, he rigged powerstats of his own, figuring out how much to dial it per frame. It was something that a computer should've been programmed to do, but it was impractical"],["39","The Howling, The Thing, Krull","CINEFEX 12 • 39"],["40","The Howling, The Thing, Krull","40 * CINEFEX 12 •"],["41","The Howling, The Thing, Krull","to bring someone in for this. We usually had two different powerstats going and a couple of orange-filtered lights hooked up to them, so there'd be some variation in different parts of the shot. I basically had to figure out the voltage for each frame, and then indicate it on a cartoon animation timing sheet. Sometimes Randy would time his action to a certain intensity of light. If he wanted to go into shadow, he'd tell me and I'd rework my flickering scheme.\" A Mitchell Standard camera was used, adapted to accept Mitchell BNC-mount lenses. Since Panavision lenses are adaptable to a BNC mount, the animation could therefore be shot anamorphic. Aupperle discovered that Panavision did have a camera geared for stop-motion, but its longest exposure was one-eighth of a second and Cook's animation required one-second exposures. Occasionally, Cook had Aupperle produce a blur on a tentacle by double-exposing two half-second exposures on one frame. This often got quite hairy, as the Acme animation motor had a tendency to throw the shutter out of whack on the backwind. \"The nice thing about the Mitchell, though,\" said Aupperle, was that it had been engineered to take lineup clips for placement in the viewing tube. All Mitchells will take clips, but usually you have to punch the frame out of the film strip. This one allowed you to line up the strip. Sometimes we had bluescreen foreground elements that had been shot earlier. Lining that up in the viewing tube was invaluable in matching a live-action shadow with the shadow on the miniature. And since we were working with prefilmed explosions, we could advance that footage frame by frame in the tube and determine how far the tentacles should be reaching to match up with the debris. It would have helped, though, if we could have had a black-and-white print made of the bluescreen action. A separation filter would have rendered the blue transparent. As it was, we only had time to get a color daily in there, and it was difficult looking through the blue on the clip.\" The situation at Hartland was unique in that all the bluescreen work was shot on a stage adjacent to the animation setup. Matte photographer Bill Taylor and director of photography Dean Cundey would set up the bluescreen shots. Randy Cook would then coordinate with them on how big the explosion should be, and Jim Aupperle would insure that the camera angle, height and tilt were proper and could be carried over into the miniature. According to Aupperle's scheme, about twenty lights were used on the miniature set, with quite a few just to pick up detail. Cook started animating the five cuts in late April, only a month before the film was scheduled to be previewed. Cut One had an animated tentacle bursting up through the floor, with foreground snow and flying boards matted in. Cut Two showed the tentacle whipping around and pulling a detonator into the hole. Cut Three was a POV shot taken from below - with the monster up against the ceiling, striking a typically menacing pose. At first the bug arms and mouth are visible; then it turns slowly, revealing a human face and arm on the other side for a horrifying effect. Bluescreen elements of falling debris were composited with it. Cut Four had the dog leaping out of the Blairmonster's stomach, landing on the ground and crawling forward - a shot of which Cook was particularly proud. Cut Five, the final one, had the animated dog reacting to the dynamite just before it blows up. \"One cut was fifteen seconds long; the others were about seven seconds each,\" Cook commented. \"It was meticulous work, because there were props and hanging bars all over that I had to work around, and snow that had to be left untouched during animation. Even manipulating the puppet was a chore; sometimes you'd have to approach it from several different sides.\" Everyone breathed easier when the animation was finished. Then the phone rang. All but the first two quick cuts - used as bluescreen background plates - were being deep-sixed.John Carpenter called me up himself and laid the news on me, rather than wait or have a secretary call me,\" Cook related, with hardly a hint of a sour grape. \"I'm sure it wasn't the most pleasant thing he had to do that day. He explained that the animation and the full-scale mechanicals just didn't work well together. And frankly, I could see that. They really didn't look very much alike. Jim Aupperle agreed that the final live and animated scenes did not match well. \"Unfortunately, we were well into the animation by the time they finished the mechanical monster, so we didn't have anything to match it to. The moves on the big one were a lot broader and more random than the ones Randy was doing. He came up with a more deliberately controlled look - one of the natures of the system. You can never control a mechanical one quite as finely.\" Gone, too, were several stop-motion visions never committed to film. Toward the end, the cook was to have disappeared into the back room. He re-enters, pleading for help - but then comes the realization that he's on the end of a tentacle which bursts right through him. Then Cook wanted to get in another bluescreen shot of MacReady stumbling back with the dynamite, away from the creature, as the camera on the animated set pulled back a bit. The set was even built with that in mind. But the idea was scrapped when Carpenter didn't feel it fit in with the cutting sequen not embittered by it at all,\" said Cook. \"John Carpenter was a very generous guy to work for. Everyone who worked on the film pushed him or herself to the limit, and came through under a lot of duress - with a minimum of complaints or problems. But it did cost me a few grey hairs. There were a lot of ideas in the backs of our minds as alternative ways to do the sequence. I never realized that one alternative would be to exclude the three final cuts. I thought it would be impossible to do that. But I'm always ready to be surprised, I guess. I just wish they'd left all my stuff in The Thing and cut all my stuff out of QI\" Not a nodding acquaintance with things, werewolves or flying serpents does The Hunger have. Permeated with the macabre sensuality of Whitley Strieber's vampirish novel, produced by Richard Shepherd and debut-directed by Tony Scott (Ridley's brother), the forthcoming M-G-M release concerns the existence of an eternally beautiful woman (Catherine Deneuve) - a memCook animates the first scene in which the beast's tentacle thrusts up through the floor boards. For added realism, a burst of snow and flying debris was bluescreened in at the moment of impact. / A low-angle shot of the Blairmonster rising to full height was one of those cut from the film when it was found that the animation puppet and the full-size mechanical creature completed afterwards did not match closely enough in appearance or movement. / Ultimately, only two of the five finished stop-motion shots were actually included in the final cut, and neither of those showed the full creature. • 41 CINEFEX 12 •"],["42","The Howling, The Thing, Krull","With a two-sided head and three arms on the torso, four undulating tentacles on the base, and a separate dogcreature on its own umbilical, the Blairmonster proved to be a challenging exercise in stopComplicating things further was the size of the miniature set and the fact that it was miniature props and hanging pipes. As a result, it was often several different angles just to motion animation. virtually littered with necessary for Cook to approach the setup from complete a single move. CINEFEX 12 42 ber of a race of ostensibly immortal beings which, like the animals in Strieber's The Wolfen, has coexisted with mankind and fed on it for survival - and the plight of her consort (David Bowie) whose internal mechanism controlling the aging process has begun to deteriorate. In a subplot sequence, rhesus monkeys caged in the laboratory of a gerontologist start behaving erratically and wrangle with each other. The male is separated from the others and, like Bowie, begins to physically deteriorate, eventually disintegrating into bones and dust. Conveyed somewhat nebulously in the script, what has happened to the monkey stands as a visual metaphor for what is happening to Bowie, to Deneuve's other male lovers, and ultimately to Deneuve herself. Dick Smith was hired to mastermind the special aging makeup effects for the human characters. The simian disintegration scene, however, was a different animal altogether. At the recommendation of Smith - and Rick Baker, who provided some initial consultation - David Allen was contracted to supply the producers with stop-motion footage of the ape withering down to nothing. \"I think Dick may have felt he had enough to worry about with his human mummies,\" Allen theorized, \"and that a visual effect was required that he didn't feel was in his bag of tricks. It was soon determined, though, that not only would they need a disintegration scene, but also shots of the caged monkeys battling each other. The word 'puppets' was mentioned. The more I realized what they wanted, though, the more I didn't think they'd be happy with puppet animation. I just didn't think it would yield the right look. I felt it would work better with mechanical puppets, and so I thought of Roger Dicken, who had worked with me on this kind of thing before on White Dog, a Sam Fuller exploitation film. Roger did the mechanical construction of the caged monkeys, and I did the taxidermy work. But my responsibility was more on the disintegration effect. That's how it broke down.\" The movies have seen this kind of effect before in stop-motion, the most graphic example being the accelerated decay of a Morlock towards the end of George Pal's The Time Machine. Technicians at Project Unlimited sculpted clay and plaster bits over a skull and picked away at it frame by frame, drooping the head at the final point of decomposition for a grand guignol effect. As eerie as that was, the requirements for Dave Allen's shot called for a much higher level of discipline and articulation. I'm tempted to say that it's impossible to do a thing like that and make it a hundred percent believable. I've seen other examples of it in films, and they always ended up being suggestions or abstraction, either through cutaways or dissolves. Some of them were great. But here, a monkey was supposed to disintegrate before your eyes, right down to nothing, leaving only the barest calcium parts. I recognized that substitutions had to take place, and that they had to be as undetectable as possible. That was going to be tough. I also recognized that shooting this would be a nightmare - and indeed, that's what it turned out to be. It was easily the longest time I'd ever spent on an actual shot - nine days, six or seven hours a day.\" Assisted by his associate, Dennis Gordon, Allen set up a primary animation camera for the master shot. After every seven or eight frames of movement, a separate backup camera recorded one-foot bursts of film at sound speed, so that in the event something happened to the main Mitchell or the film buckled, Allen would at least have major moments of the sequence which, as a last resort, could be dissolved together for an ersatz effect. A third camera was employed specifically for facial closeups. The actual inspiration for slugging this sequence into the film was a time-lapse demonstration of a rat disintegrating, which had been aired on Nova. \"I happen to know that Richard Shepherd and Tony Scott looked at that footage before I was hired,\" Allen divulged. \"I presume the idea was that I could deliver something like that. The problem there was one of maggot infestation, giving the rat a strange life of its own - an obvious nervousness. Then, as the ligaments ossified, it produced all kinds of pops and jerks. Naturally none of that was acceptable for The Hunger. I think the end result the producer was hoping for was to have the audience believe that we actually went out and shot a dead monkey in time-lapse.\" Allen's taxidermy skills have figured prominently in a number of films, including The Howling, White Dog, and his unfinished epic, The Primevals. For The Hunger, he employed synthetic orlon hair due to the scarcity of the real thing. (Real monkey hair was employed on a separate figure which ended up not being used.) \"The process I use is similar to what George Lofgren did on Mighty Joe Young,\" Allen explained, \"and it works with both real and synthetic hair. First, you take any water-soluble substance and comb it into the hair. Once that's set off, you remove the backing, be it tanned hide or cloth. The intention is to expose some of the hair from the medium it's suspended in. Then you coat it with rubber cement or foam latex. When the rubber solidifies, you wash the water-soluble medium out of the hair and you've got a flexible backing. To me, one disadvantage of Lofgren's system is that you're stuck with the original length of the hair. Many times I like to vary the shag of the hair. I can do that by razor-cutting from below and regulating the amount of hair to be used above the flexible material. The theoretical advantage of Lofgren's technique, though, is that the hair follicle stays better in the backing, since you have the swelled follicle end which makes it less susceptible to falling out. Lofgren's system, which capitalized on the ability of dermestid beetles to clean the hide off by infestation, is particularly good when dealing with hair of very short length. Sometimes you literally want it just like the hair around the nose of a dog.\" Allen also developed a way of impregnating the hide with a thin sheet of malleable lead. After the hair was fixed in the rubber, he glued the lead to the underside, whipped up another batch of foam and spread a thin coat of it onto the lead. For the disintegrating monkey, he essentially made a large firm coat in two or three sections to envelop the corpse.iThat gave me a couple of options to kick the sequence off. Since there was a lot of air under the musculature, I could crush the lead-lined fur to sug-"],["43","The Howling, The Thing, Krull","gest withering during animation. Then, at a certain point, I could cut it, the lead being laminated in between. From there, I could pull it back like tin foil and reveal the inside - sort of a stylized abstraction of the way something actually disintegrates. In fact, most real disintegration occurs with the hair almost mummifying over the corpse. But I didn't want to have a situation where the thing just withered with the fur gluing up next to the bone - especially knowing that I'd still have to get rid of it somehow. So, during the shot, the fur was being cut away as well as being folded back.\" Once the internal structure was exposed, the muscles and ligaments, made of plastic urethane, were made to disintegrate and retreat by applying heat. Pulling this shot off credibly was not merely a matter of picking away at the puppet at a steady pace. The fleshy parts of the face had to wither and change while the fur mummified at a certain rate. Allen realized that dissolves were needed to alter the head up to the point of it mummifying; and to do so, the head and body would have to be filmed in separate exposures for a certain frame count. \"That was where the third camera setup came in. I made a matte and countermatte of the face area. Then I had a little rubber mask that could fit over the skull like a glove. The lead-backed head - the mummified face that the rubber turns into - could also slip over the skull. I then shot a lot of footage of just the face with the countermatte in place, took it away, and used a 4x5 camera so I could mark off all the main points of registration on the rubber face. Then the two masks were dissolved into each other. Once that was done, I removed the countermatte, backwound the camera, popped in the face matte and printed the monkey's body back in. When you come out of the dissolve, you're in a full exposure situation, with no mattes or dissolves. Originally the dissolve was two feet long, but the producers later extended it to four or five feet by doubling up on the frames in the printer. I did another dissolve where I slowly removed the color from the face. At first it was flesh colored; then it went to a blue-gray. That was also done within the matte. The matte was run soft so I could use the edges to feather out the lead-lined face and create the softness of the hairline.\" Some of the skeletal parts were those of a real monkey obtained from a lab. When the parts were judged too small for the scale of the puppet, Allen cut them in half, drilled pins in the end of each bone and filled in the gaps with putty and bondo, extending them. He then took molds off the revised bones and cast them in a bicarbonated plaster that crumbles easily. These were used for the substitutions at the end of the scene, with Allen animating bits of the skeleton falling to the floor on wires and then dissolving the bicarbonated bones in, pulverizing them a frame at a time. ¿It became like a can of worms. You realized that you ought to be animating everything, which is almost mathematically impossible.\" After spending nine grueling days aging a desiccated ape into nothingness, Dave Allen unloaded his three cameras and the film was developed and edited. Allen was pleased with the results. Not surprisingly, the clinker came in the reaction of the producers. Since no parameters were ever specified as to the CINEFEX 12 • 43"],["44","The Howling, The Thing, Krull","44 * CINEFEX 12"],["45","The Howling, The Thing, Krull","length of the shot, Allen had used his own judgment and delivered a scene that lasted approximately seven seconds. The producers claimed that they wanted a scene that ran for twenty-five seconds. Quite a stipulation to be made, after the fact. \"There was no way that a figure three-and-a-half feet long could have been animated for twenty-five seconds,\" Allen maintained. You're talking about hundreds and hundreds of frames in which you are, in effect, animating the whole thing. I had no way of subdividing the action any finer. I think I would've lost all kinds of accuracy and control. I worked at the speed the system would work best.\" In the end, Richard Shepherd extended Allen's footage by slipping the sync on the exposure, an optical process whereby any frame in a given shot becomes composed of a frame coming before and after. \"It's like a lap dissolve per frame,\" Allen explained. \"It smooths out action much better than double- or trible-framing a piece of film to make it longer. The last report was that they were going to slip the sync three or four frames per frame. That could hurt things a bit. Some of it didn't look bad on the tests I saw, especially when the fur starts to wither at the start of the sequence. But when the bones were falling in animation, it's not appropriate. You see half exposures of bones traveling downward. I wanted them to drop in and out of that slip-sync mode, but they didn't do it. I now hear that they've done an optical blowup on it and that it will be used on a television monitor, possibly in black-and-white, which will degrade it even further. I suppose it's just as well. At any rate, the results should be interesting, even if it's just another example of the not-quite-right Most recently, Allen has completed stop-framing the final scene of a nameless creature on the wing of a plane for George Miller's new Nightmare at 20,000 Feet segment of The Twilight Overseas, at Pinewood Studios near London town, new animated wonders have been brewing. Krull is Columbia's megabuck sword-and-sorcery gamble for 1983, marking director Peter Yates' first excursion into the genre. Among its grabbag of visual effects are a swirling five-bladed glaive, on-camera transformations, firemares and a giant crystalline spider monster - all of which have contributed to a protracted release date. Set in medieval times on another planet, the film has been called by insiders a cross between Beauty and the Beast and Robin Hood. The film easily invites comparison with any of Ray Harryhausen's recent efforts, particularly Clash of the Titans with its decrepit hags, magic sword and the inevitable \"quest.\" Krull's producers were keenly aware of this - one orphan of the script, a Snake Lady concept designed for an actress, was written out because of its similarity to Harryhausen's Medusa. The centerpiece of Krull's bag of tricks is the crystal spider that keeps the Widow of the Web captive in a cocoon. The story point proved fortuitous for young Steven Archer, Ray Harryhausen's apprentice animator on Clash of the Titans, who was hired to render it in stop-motion.i After Clash, I was looking for a job and contacted Derek Meddings,\" Archer stated. \"I told him I had done some animation and asked him if he was looking for anyone in his department. And he said, actually, that they were looking for someone to animate a spider for The Dragons of Krull - which was the original title. I showed him some drawings, together with photos of my films and two models I had made, and kept in contact with him. In September 1981, he called me to work on the film.\" Meddings, Krull's overall effects supervisor, had stop-motion in mind from the start and felt it was the only way the spider could be presented realistically. At first, Archer was called upon to test-shoot a scene of two characters throwing axes at each other. The axes were to interlock, fall and pin another character to the ground. Meddings' confidence in Archer was reinforced by his success with the scene. \"I did another test of an actor throwing an ax towards the camera. As he'd throw the real ax, he'd drop it behind him and continued the action. That was shot as a plate in VistaVision. We front-projected it and combined it with a miniature ax which was about a foot long and suspended on thin wires from a wooden track above. Since it had to rotate, we had to change the wires around. The shot lasted only a second. Then I moved onto the Based on the anatomy of a black widow, Archer's arachnid was built by Derek Medding's model unit out of clear perspex - the English equivalent of plexiglass. The abdomen featured a red hourglass shape that tied in with the sequence, since a real hourglass, inverted by the haggard Widow of the Web, mobilizes the spider. Originally the entire abdomen was to turn cherry-red when the spider was angered, but the idea was dropped for technical reasons and just the internal hourglass shape remained. Problem: How does one hide the connecting joints in a transparent stop-motion model? \"We had to use wire,\" Archer recalled somewhat wearily, \"because the legs were so thin. When Derek's people were building it, they asked me about the joints - what the tine l would be the best thing for them. At the time, I would have preferred very small ball-and-sockets for the legs; but they were unable to do that, except where the legs joined the body. Those particular joints were made of perspex. We had little grub screws that would tighten them, but we found that they would cause the joints to wear. Often, we'd have to dismantle them and get new sections redone. For the leg sections, we used tin-copper wire - several strands twisted together. Unfortunately, the wire made for problems; you just can't get very subtle movement with it. You have to move it two inches to get one inch of movement, because it tends to spring back. After a few days, the wire would ew day get away with th. break. If the spider was walking fast, you could get away with it. Eventually the legs would have to be removed and the wire repaired. That's something I would not like to do again!' The spider model covered quite an area. Each leg was about a foot long, and the body itself was over a foot long. Thus, with legs outstretched, the spider measured three feet. The legs were made of solid perspex, but the abdomen was kept hollow. Archer saw to it that the head had movement capabilities. It also had two movable feeler claws and eight black eyes made of plastic. For The Hunger, David Allen was contracted to manufacture a full-size rhesis monkey and then photograph it decomposing to dust in a single uninterrupted take. Starting with a real skeleton - much of which he recast in bicarbonated plaster so it would crumble easily - Allen built up internal organs and tissue from plastic urethane that would disintegrate when heat was applied. Artificial fur was affixed to thin lead sheets and carefully shaped into proper form. Withering the flesh and picking away at it one frame at a time proved extremely arduous, and Allen spent a full nine days on the seven-second cut. 45 CINEFEX 12 •"],["46","The Howling, The Thing, Krull","Titans - was given his first solo assignment on Krull, animating a giant crystal assistant position spider and web for a test shot. / Effects supervisor Derek Meddings dresses the wire web with wisps of angel hair. / Archer solders a section of the web prior to an early test. The spider's red abdomen was crimson hourglass. / The crystal spider on her web. Steven Archer - Ray Harryhausen's young assistant on Clash of the spider. / Archer and an later replaced by a transparent one with a 46 - CINEFEX 12 Body hairs - made of fiberglass - would flex and retreat to their original position during animation; and they served to disguise the joints, as well. Of course, the spider had to walk on a web, a situation that can tax any animator's brain cells. Archer wound up making the web out of tin-copper wire, braiding it heavily to support the weight of the spider. \"There's no special reason for that particular wire. It's just what happened to be around at the time.\" The web was dressed with angel hair. At first, Archer animated the spider on a web of pure wire - with the plan then being to overlay the hair optically, since Archer was worried about it moving during the scene.iOn the first two shots I did, they were going to do the overlay, but the tests didn't work out. We used angel hair in the background on some of these shots, but it wasn't actually connected to the web. Later on, we got a bit more adventurous and started sticking it to the wire. We applied it with glue, which lacquered it down but still made it look delicate. At times, we used hair spray. The angel hair would also help disguise the wire, An overhead track was used to hang an aerial brace, which in turn held the weight of the spider, supporting it on the web. One of Meddings' stipulations, however, was that the web had to sag as the creature traversed it. In response, Archer devised a system whereby minute winching devices would pull the web down in increments, using monofilament fishing line. The line was barely visible, and with the complex visual scheme of wire lattice and angel hair, no special pains were taken to disguise it. \"It was a rather busy setup,\" Archer pointed out. \"I had four thin wires suspending the spider from above, and eight wires from below. pendin ather pider Because of all of that, it took quite a while to turn out footage - at least two or three days to do a shot. Because the web was so fragile, we had to have the door closed, and we couldn't even have a fan blowing air around. It got very hot in therel When I animated, I had to step aside and wait for the web to settle before shooting it. When I first did the test, during which the web was made only of wire, the heat of the lamps actually made the thing move about. So we had to be very careful.\" All of the spider action takes place in a deep cavern within a a pain. The p hollow mountain. The hero's seer-friend, Ynyr, seeks out the Widow of the Web for information on how to overcome the evildoers. Since the Widow is confined to her cocoon at the center of the web, Ynyr crawls along the inner strand in an effort to reach her. The crystal spider is on the opposite side of the web, feeling for vibrations with outstretched legs. Sensing the intrusion, it crawls toward the center, waiting for Ynyr to exit. Angrily, it tries to ensnare him, but fails. Ynyr escapes from the cave; and the spider, being too large to go through the cave entrance, vents its rage by climbing on top of the cocoon in an effort to smash it to bits. There the sequence ends. To provide cave backgrounds for the spider setup, Derek Meddings' team photographed 8x10 color transparencies of a miniature cave setting and blew them up to a height of ten feet. The technique was the same one Meddings used for many shots in the Superman films. The transparencies were backlit with a"],["47","The Howling, The Thing, Krull","CINEFEX 12 • 47"],["48","The Howling, The Thing, Krull","battery of twenty lights.iYou have to be very careful with the way the transparencies are lit and the camera is angled,\" Archer cautioned.iOn the first shots we did, we had reflections of the spider because of its shiny perspex surface. Sometimes we had to spray it to diffuse the background a bit and kill off any reflections. I believe that shot is used, anyway; it was quite wideangled. But just as the spider walks across the web, you can start to see the reflection.\" Surprisingly, only one shot had the stop-motion spider and human actor in the same scene. The approach to the shot turned out to be a change of pace for Archer, who had spent so much time working with Harryhausen's setups of animating in front of process screens. Rather than going that route, Meddings designed his one composite shot much the way Willis O' Brien had done, by projecting images into a miniature set. Although a large cocoon and cave set-piece had been built on one of Krull's ten stages, erecting them in full size for long shots would have been a massive undertaking. \"On that one shot,\" said Archer, \"I animated the spider walking from right to left, in VistaVision. That was used as a plate which was front-projected into the miniature set. It was quite a big miniature. They projected my plate up, and another plate of Ynyr, together, and rephotographed that. The good thing about it was that you could project the plates and refilm them at twenty-four frames per second - in real time. So the web moving about only added to the realism of the setup. There were a number of other front projection shots of Ynyr in the cave without the spider, as well. \"In the first script, Ynyr was going to catch the spider and stab it in the back. Then the hero, Colwyn, was to enter and throw his glaive, which was supposed to hit the spider and push it into the cocoon, smashing the whole thing into a thousand pieces. For some reason, they just changed it; but we did have a breakaway spider built for that shot - with just wire in there for all the joints. I ended up using it as a backup model. If one of the legs broke on the first model, I could always switch to the other while it was being repaired. Interestingly, there were no full sections of the spider built; it was all animation.\" On nearly all of Archer's spider animation cuts, one or two Panavision cameras were in use, plus the VistaVision camera used for plate photography. All three were often employed simultaneously in order to provide the editor with extra spider footage, and to disguise the same animation by cutting to different angles. Twenty spider scenes were planned. Archer wound up doing only ten. For one specific cut, Archer and company rigged their own motion control unit. \"When we first see the spider coming out of this crack in the cave, it strokes the web, feeling for vibrations. Then it emerges in this tracking shot for which we used a computer. The camera could pan, track and tilt. It was a lot easier for the computer to work out the moves. Plotting out and marking them by hand is quite a headache. So we used a computer in just that one shot, where the camera tracks through the web and the spider turns around, inverting, dropping down on this thread and walking toward the camera. There were several shots of the 48 - CINEFEX 12"],["49","The Howling, The Thing, Krull","spider walking right into the camera, with the camera tracking with him - shots that were storyboarded, but ones I was never asked to do. I think they got just 'bare minimum' of the spider.\" In addition, two bluescreen traveling matte shots of the spider were filmed. In one, the creature comes slowly into view, watching Ynyr climb toward the cocoon. The other was a closer angle of the same. \"I don't know if they'll be used,\" said the animator. They haven't been married to the background yet. But the rest of the shots used the huge transparencies.\" The final cuts in the spider sequence took the longest to animate. Here, the creeping menace smashes the crystal cocoon, and everything shatters to bits. Archer rigged twenty aerial wires, five for each cone of the cocoon. The falling debris had to be animated frame by frame, using the near-masochistic technique Ray Harryhausen has employed down through the years. Some of the spider scenes were so wide-angle, Archer and his assistants had to build a large arm for surface gauges to swing in on, and a special removable platform for other gauges, simply because there was no place to hide anything. \"In a rear projection setup, you're dealing with an area of five feet or so. Here, it was perhaps fifteen feet. I think I spent sixty days on the animation for Krull, over a period of six months. I made the final cut of the cocoon last thirteen seconds, expanding it for as long as possible because animation sometimes cuts very quickly. I spent eight-and-a-half days on the last shot. Frankly, I was quite worried about how it would turn out. Fortunately, we had a singleframe video machine. After a day's work, I'd study the action and weed out any problems. It's a shame, really, that it wasn't developed into a fight sequence,\" Archer lamented. \"That's always been one of the more interesting things - hero fighting animated creature. But there is none of that. It's just the threat of the spider being there. It could've been done; there was no problem with that. Originally the sequence was supposed to be about ten minutes long, but I believe it's been edited down to two or three minutes. For some reason they just changed it, and I don't really know why.\" After the spider, Archer moved on to other bits of business. He animated the actual shot of the flying axes in front of a miniature front projection; and the Cyclops' trident was stop-framed in the same manner. One or two shots of the five-bladed glaive were also animated, but most of it was done as a stage effect, with a prop actually being flung on wires. Other glaive shots were done on a bluescreen motion control rig, just to create the impression of it flying by. The Corridor of Bones that ensnares the female lead was a miniature set shot as a bluescreen background, with the camera tracking back - not only on the miniature, but on the princess as well. As the story progresses, Princess Lyssa is kidnapped by the evil Slayers - the 'stormtroopers\" of the nameless Beast and guardians of the Black Fortress. According to Archer, the Slayers were originally conceived as animation models - at least for their death scenes.iThey wanted something unusual, but as it turned out, I never made the three-foot Slayer models. The armatures were started by Norman Kersh, who sadly died during the production of frull. Instead, the death scene was realized in live-action by using replicas of the costumes. In one explicit moment, a Slayer's head is split open, revealing a worm-like creature that burrows itself into the ground. \"When animation was being considered, this had not been decided on, because with animation, you could go anywhere you'd want to go. In the scene where Ergo the magician turns into a tiger, I suggested that one shot be done in animation, or perhaps several shots - but they changed that sequence, too.\" Krull is permeated throughout with an assortment of other effects outside the stop-motion arena. Optical supervisor Robin Browne designed a scene where the Widow of the Web transforms from her withered self into a beautiful young girl, while her body is in motion - and in front of a mirror to boot. The magician changes into a dog, an effect achieved by retouching blowups of VistaVision frames of man and dog and blending them together on the animation stand, courtesy of artist Peter Chiang, Laser weapons - originally conceived as glowing neon arrows - were executed by Jamie Harcourt under the direction of Derek Meddings. Firemares with flaming hooves are ridden by Prince Colwyn and his minions toward the Black Fortress in order to reach Lyssa in time. i\"They actually made a mechanical horse with fireemitting hooves,\" Archer revealed, \"but I don't think they can use those shots because they moved quite jerkily. Now they are filming the flames separately and optically matching them to the hooves - and there are seven or eight of these horses moving at very fast speeds against fantasy backgrounds! Of course, they've got to be shot bluescreen. All this has resulted in the film being moved back to August. When I left last October, they still had the firemares to complete and several traveling matte shots to do. As the film is progressing, they're coming up with additional effects, adding to the workload! It's a vicious circle.\" The most provocative news for young Archer at this point in time is his imminent involvement with The Never Ending Story, a $25 million extravaganza set for release around Christmas of 1983. The film, under Das Boot director Wolfgang Petersen, represents the most expensive European-based picture ever made. British special effects are being assembled by Brian Johnson, whose brainstorm for the film is the largest blue screen ever built - fifty feet high, thirty-five feet wide. Colin Arthur, of Clash of the Titans fame, is designing special makeups and creatures. And Steven Archer is preparing to animate the Lucksdragon, which on the screen will appear to loom forty-five feet high. \"I think I'll be on it for about a year,\" Archer said with a puckish glint of anticipation. \"It's set in a complete fantasy environment, rather like Dark Crystal. It's about a little boy who steps through a book he's reading into this world which he's forced to save from destruction. At the moment, I believe the dragon has feathers! And there's a rather endearing giant snail with a shell like a mountain. There's the character writing the story, and the characters in the story - hence, the title. Brian Johnson told me that I would be on the film until July or August of 1983. I'm rather excited about it.\" Archer and an assistant make adjustments to the wire web. The crisscrossed spider's cave - extremely large for a stopmotion set - often made access to the puppet difficult, and thereby extended the animation process considerably. One particularly complex shot required eightand-a-half days to complete. / Archer animates the spider approaching her cocoon. During photography, the spider - which was too heavy to support its own weight - was suspended on wires from above. Additional wires connected to the web were attached to small winching devices and drawn down from below to create a sagging effect as the spider walked across. CINEFEX 12 • 49"],["50","Dream Quest","A Dream in the Making"],["51","Dream Quest","Once upon a time, in the realm of Imagination, a band of young Image Magicians set out to establish a small stronghold on the frontier of the fx world. Three years of trials have passed since these six wizards joined forces, yet there is no telling when their story might end and their legend begin - the legend of the DREAM QUESTI If you are a movie patron who remains riveted to the screen until the final credit has crawled, these names may hold some significance: Scott Squires, Hoyt Yeatman, Rocco Gioffre, Frederick Iguchi, Thomas Hollister, Robert Hollister. For the most part, however, very few people are aware of the true extent of their combined contributions to contemporary cinema, and fewer still know that these individuals are the principal partners in the Dream Quest special effects facility. They don't advertise very often, and their demo reel is seldom up-to-date. This is because they are too busy working - making films better by making quality special effects more cost effective, and thus more accessible to more producers for use in a wider variety of projects. Dream Quest is doing business in an unusually practical and exciting fashion in the hinterlands of Hollywood. Before there was a Dream Quest, there were six individuals who loved motion pictures. Scott Squires was a typical child of technology - a member of his Indiana school's lighting and audiovisual crews and the yearbook photographer. With some college credit and scholastic awards in these areas, he turned his credentials into jobs as a theater projectionist, TV stringer, industrial filmmaker and newspaper photographer. He came to Hollywood in 1975 to take the assistant cameraman's test, but - fortunately, in retrospect - failed to win one of the ten positions open. Instead, he went to work as an assistant wedding photographer while knocking on every door in town. Persistence paid off. In November, he was referred to Future General where an assistant was needed with a broad base of knowledge in photography, animation and related areas. \"When I met Doug Trumbull for the first time,\" Squires said, \"he seemed pleased with me even without looking at my films. Future General saw me as someone who could be handed a number of different types of problems and do a lot of research for solutions. They said I would start in two weeks; but by the time I got home, they had changed their minds. I was to report for work in three days!\" Squires was hired initially to work on Trumbull's dark-ride project for amusement parks, but instead found himself immediately involved in research and development on Close Encounters of the Third Kind.The first day there they asked me to produce clouds in a liquid. They knew it was possible; after all, when you put cream in your coffee you get great-looking clouds. But reallooking clouds in a clear medium? That's the first thing they threw at me - a twenty-gallon aquarium and twenty dollars from petty cash.\" Squires kept copious notes, concentrating on compounds that had to be both cheap and readily available, without being toxic, corrosive or unstable. It took Squires only a week to work out the basic process that has become an industry standard for creating clouds - the injection of white Tempera paint into the inversion layer between salt water and fresh water in a lighted tank. He continued as Trumbull's assistant, developing devices and techniques for Close Encounters, the dark-ride system and, later, the demo for Trumbull's Super 70 process. He became a camera assistant on Close Encounters, working with Dennis Muren on the mothership, the music lights on the ship, starfields and the Big Dipper sequence. Squires also did miniature photography for the Buck Rogers feature and Battlestar Galactica series at Universal, then survived the shakeup on Star Trek - The Motion Picture, starting with the Abel team, and staying on when his mentor, Trumbull, took the project over. It was a fast four years for this young visionary from Indiana - nonstop work until the conclusion of Star Trek. It was January 1980, the Trumbull team was again disbanded, and Squires was looking for the next challenge In June 1975, while Squires was testing for cameraman, high school student Rocco Gioffre was in Lorain, Ohio, reading one of those \"hometown-boy-makes-good\" articles in the local paper. It was about that premier matte artist, Matthew Yuricich. Gioffre, a devout film buff who had been shooting his own mattes and stop-motion puppets for his school's art class, was inspired enough and brave enough to send Yuricich some of his drawings and paintings. \"I just asked him for some advice,\" Gioffre pointed out.i\"Then I got to meet him when he came home to Ohio for a visit.\" Yuricich remembers that first encounter.Rocco had written me letters, and I got him some things he needed, like frontprojection screens. I was impressed by the fact that he was doing matte paintings right there at home. They weren't red-hot, but he was doing all of it. He had a damn good knowledge of this stuff. So when I got busy on Close Encounters, I talked to Doug Trumbull about this kid I wanted to train as a matte artist, and he said sure. Doug's always ready to help. Rocco proved to be a hell of a hard worker, and is now probably the most knowledgeable matte man in the business when it comes to knowing the camera and how to get the most out of his mattes on film.\" \"Assistant matte painter' is basically a glorified title for brush cleaner and scraper-upper,\" Gioffre said with a smile. After some colone and. before it wa cleaning and scraping, he started rotoscoping and, before it was over, contributed two matte paintings to Close Encounters - the city lights and the runway lights. Afterward, Gioffre worked with Yuricich on mattes for Damnation Alley, The Deer Hunter and other films. The young artist worked on the Oxberry stand at the Hartland facility, doing garbage mattes for Buck Rogers and Galactica, and finally earned his first screen credit while working with Yuricich on Star Trek. One of the shots he worked on was a rendering of the Enterprise when Spock's Vulcan shuttle does a half-gainer flipover and backs toward the bridge of the giant starship.iA lot of people thought that was the miniature Enterprise, but they went to a painting because they couldn't get the necessary depth of field when they were that tight on the article by model.\" Was he proud to see his name on the silver screen? \"Oh, Marc A. Richardson sure, but Matt has worked on a number of shows and not gotten"],["52","Dream Quest","One of Dream Quest's early feature assignments involved producing a motion-control graphics for Escape from New York. Two stylized cityscapes contrast stock by the Dream Quest team. / Hoyt Yeatman adjusts his lighting for a highangle pass over Manhattan. / For closer views, a snorkel lens was employed to sweep down between the miniature buildings. / The final results - suggestive of vector-image reconnaissance photography simulation of computer were constructed by Mark Stetson and then photographed on high- - were enhanced with animation readouts. 52 - CINEFEX 12 any credit. He negotiates it when he can, but he's more interested in doing the work than quibbling over credit, and I have always felt the same way - the work is what counts.\" Slightly older than Squires and Gioffre, and imbued with even greater chutzpah, Hoyt Yeatman might have missed the most significant opportunity of his life had he not had a friend working as a production assistant on Close Encounters. Yeatman was still at UCLA, doing a film in the animation workshop. His project dealt with front projection and miniature puppet animation - a complex project which created problems the professors just couldn't answer. To find those answers, Yeatman turned to the people at Future General. Robert Swarthe, who was animation supervisor on Close Encounters, still laughs pleasantly at the memory.iHoyt came in from UCLA - this student with his little suitcase and a pocket computer that he had turned into a camera controller interlocked with his little Bolex. I was totally amazed. He kept coming around; and eventually he got the night-shift job.\" Dennis Muren and Scott Squires were busy creating the mothership landing sequence. Working long hours, they still had to run all night to meet deadlines. Yeatman was there, with bells on. \"During the last month of school, I worked the graveyard - from 7 p.m. to 7 a.m. The passes I was babysitting were up to seven minutes per frame! I would sit all night with a ten-watt bulb in a totally dark room. It was so dark, many times I didn't even know what I was shooting!\" School and Close Encounters finished at the same time, but Yeatman did not remain idle. Mad magazine artist Sergio Aragones saw his award-winning student film, Canned Performance, and hired him to work on one shot for a new Laugh-In series for television. That one shot turned into six months of work, followed by commercial work as technical director for Robert Abel, miniature photography for Universal's Buck Rogers and Galactica, and, finally, a call-back from Trumbull to work on the motion control system for the Close Encounters - Special SEE Edition and Star Trek. Yeatman was delighted. The 'Icebox' was a unique system, and I was one of the few people who knew how to run it. I worked alongside Dave Stewart, a real great in the business. This was the first time I really got to work on something, eventually producing shots on the big stage, with a seventy-two foot track for model photographyl\" At the end of the Star Trek project, now a union cameraman with special skills in a hot new effects market, Hoyt Yeatman had some ambitious At the other end of the calendar, Robert Hollister has been in the film business since 1950. He was a twenty-eight-year-old glazier who got a reference to Technicolor and was accepted for the negative department. It was three-strip in those days, and before they let you touch a piece of film, you would spend a week just winding and rewinding the same thousand-foot reel of black leader. At the end of each day, you turned it in and an inspector would circle every scratch on the leader; and at the end of the week, if there were less than twelve scratches, you got hired. I had three, and that's how I made it into show biz.\" Hollister went"],["53","Dream Quest","CINEFEX 12 • 53 boUt STANDARD VIEW"],["54","Dream Quest","on to work at DeLuxe negative lab, then over to Disney on the original Mickey Mouse Club, where he worked next door to the current president of Local 659 of the International Photographers Union, Frank Stanley. Stanley shared his knowledge and love of cameras with me,\" Hollister recollected. \"I got interested enough to spend my own time going to machine shops where cameras were made.\"i Using this new-found passion to secure a valuable position in the M-G-M optical effects department, Hollister worked his way up to an assistant cameraman on The Man from U.N.C.L.E. and other M-G-M television series. Later he became a production camera assistant on such shows as Gunsmoke, The Mary Tyler Moore Show, Medical Center and Little House on the Prairie. Then his special knowledge of 65mm cameras got him a call to work as lead camera technician in charge of all special effects cameras on Close Encounters. He was likewise in demand by Robert Abel and Associates during their days on Star Trek - The Motion Picture, eventually following that project when it shifted over to the Trumbull organization. Though impressed with Abel's creativity, Hollister was happier to be working once again with the Trumbull-Yuricich team, and felt another pang when that team disbanded at the end of 1979. Still, Robert Hollister could count on a continuing demand for his services in the Eighties. Thomas Hollister was a child of the Sixties, graduating from high school in 1967 and going to work in his uncle's heavy construction company for a year. Then Tom's father, Robert, told him of an opening at the M-G-M film lab. Young Hollister landed the position, earning as he learned about many aspects of film processing and optical printing. M-G-M even gave him leave to serve in the Marines for two years in Vietnam, and welcomed him back after his hitch was up. When he finally left M-G-M, Hollister had the opportunity to interview at Future General at a time when they were about to lose their black-and-white processing technician. \"I was retained to set up, staff, and operate the traveling matte processing facility for 35mm and 65mm black-and-white negatives and positives. From all that I learned at M-G-M, it was really very simple and fun, so I got that going and while I was there I learned how to assist on matte camera photography and how to line up opticals. We all learned many new skills on Close EncountersiHollister went on to set up similar processing operations for Universal's Hartland effects facility, Robert Abel's Astra Image group, and was invited to stay on when Trumbull assumed responsibility for Star Trek. Frederick Iguchi has always had a clear course to follow. At Northrop Institute of Technology and in the U.S. Air Force, he developed considerable expertise in electronics, which he put to excellent use in the M-G-M Labs, designing computer interfaces for Oxberry optical printers, Bell & Howell high-speed printers, and making continuous improvements on the many hardware systems at M-G-M. After years of dependable and impressive effort, Iguchi was made electronics maintenance shift supervisor at their film labs, where he continued to upgrade and interface film devices with computers. There he met Tom Hollister, and it was that relationship - and Iguchi's incredible inventiveness - 54 - CINEFEX 12"],["55","Dream Quest","which led to his participation in the Paramount Star Trek engineering group, designing and maintaining servo-drives, stepper-drives, temperature control systems, analog and digital interfaces, and all the number-crunching devices that were fast becoming mandatory elements in contemporary film. Regrets are inescapable, even when one's personal satisfaction comes from the problem-solving side. But Fred guchi was about to experience cinematic elation as accompaniment to a job well done - in January 1980, he went to work as electronics designer for miniatures supervisor Greg Jein on the Close Encounters - Special Edition. \"I worked with Robin Leyden on the 'Blinkatron' system, which was the programmable light-intensity controller for the 'beehive' cluster in the center of the mothership. Scott Squires wrote the software for that. I interfaced the Icebox system to the large miniature they had, and built the electronics system that operated the model in the smokeroom.\" At the conclusion of the Special Edition, however, there was no specific job waiting for Iguchi. The future was a welcome mystery. During the course of Star Trek, Scott Squires, Hoyt Yeatman and Rocco Gioffre began formulating a plan to deal with the new decade. \"We discussed how neat it would be,\" Gioffre related, to rent a place together to live and work for our own entertainment and the possibility of odd jobs between the layoffs. A lot of landlords thought we were kinda kooky asking to see the garage before the house, but after all, housing is fairly standard - we wanted to see the workspace. By the time we found the right twoand-a-half car garage we wanted, Dream Quest, Incorporated was in existence with all six original members.\" Together, they had all the talent but none of the tools. Still, because the effects community is so small and their work record so strong, opportunity was continually knocking at their garage door. Apogee contracted two matte paintings for Caddyshack; Peter Donen at Cinema Research recommended Dream Quest for mattes in First Family and The Final Conflict: graphics and inserts from Jay Teitzell for the CBS Fall '80 campaign; slitscan for live-action in the Canadian Evilspeak feature; motion control for a Timex ad by Roger Flint; processing for Disney; and a steady flow of odds and ends for commercials and features. Adhering to their \"extra 10%\" work ethic and a severe policy of austerity for maximum reinvestment, Dream Quest acquired the tools of their trade at a rapid pace. Their commitment was so strong and their workload so demanding that, before a year had passed, Dream Quest signed a lease on a commercial building in Culver City, just south of Hollywood. No longer could it be called a hobby. Their last job in the garage was for John Wash of Motion Graphics, simulating computer graphics by enhancing a motion control pass of a black-and-white city miniature for Escape from New York. Their first job in the new facility entailed simulating video graphics for Blade Runner. Fred Iguchi was unable to hide a rueful smile when recalling those salad days.iReal tough. There were days we questioned whether we could meet our payroll. Nobody was of a business mind; we just knew what we wanted and everybody did everything he could to get it. We were rather naive at the start.\" Robert Swarthe, among other clients, saw it differently: \"It's amazing just how well they laid out their building - how maniacally clever, with all those double doors all over the place so if they need to extend any track, they just open up and add on.\" Indeed, a tour of the facility reveals very little \" dead\" space - a small office, a refrigerator and coffee station in the hall. The screening room is split between a conference table and some antique church pews for screenings. There is a Sony 3/3⁄4-inch videocassette player for commercial clients and an ancient 35mm Simplex projector from Yeatman's hometown theater. Editorial has simple film racks, loop racks, rewinds and a Moviola. There is a Tobias densitometer, a Bell & Howell hot splicer, and another of Yeatman's hand-crafted creations - a standard Acme projection head with an aerial movement in it and two translator tables on either side. It is used as a 1:1 bench printer optical camera, and with a beam-splitter assembly, it becomes a stopmotion process projector. It can also be rigged to fly on the motion control systems. Gioffre who often works on two or more film projects at once, stores his old paintings wherever possible. His current masterpieces are created in a humid loft, then brought downstairs by elevator to the matte camera room. The matte stand is a multiplane system, with a computer now running many of the gag devices that bring Gioffre's paintings to life. \"We use the moving clouds technique in matte painting developed by Albert Whitlock - that subliminal 'something' that might otherwise be noticeable if it wasn't there. For the foreseeable future, in locked-off shots, or scenes with just pan or tilt involved, you can't beat the speed and economy of a matte painting.\" The facility's processor is the same one bought by Columbia in 1974. Tom Hollister had set it up for Close Encounters, and persuaded the studio to sell it at the end of the project. It is a HustonFearless microfilm processor with a sprocketless demand-drive, which pulls the film between rollers. With a serpentine setup and a straight-through film path, it can run anything from 8mm to 108mm-wide film, whether perforated or not. With a special microprocessor-based motor drive to control the speed of the film as it slithers through the soup, very critical densities can be held while running positive or negative.iThis is a very special machine,\" Hollister boasted like a proud father. \"We can run from 5369 all the way down to the 5235 separation stock. And we don't have to settle for .25 or .10; if a low-density matte has to be .19, it is .19.\" With that kind of accuracy, and a set of dip tanks for quick-and-dirty matte-line wedge tests, the processor room is at the hub of the Dream Quest stages. There is a light-duty shop, which Greg Jein currently uses for his model work. For those items not available off-the-shelf, Tom Hollister uses a friend's machine shop to construct the mounts, pan/tilt heads, and other motion control accessories too large for their in-house shop. The biggest headache in all motion control work is repeatability, which is significantly determined by the quality and stability of the track on which the half-ton camera rigs roll. \"We have the For a nighttime approach to the island of Gorm in First Family, Dream Quest traveled to White's Point, north of San Pedro, and photographed an underexposed day-for-night film plate. / Retaining the water and the actual rocky coastline for maximum authenticity, Rocco Gioffre rendered the rest of the scene as a matte painting."],["56","Dream Quest","supervisor Robert Swarthe, Dream Quest photographed Greg Jein's elaborate casino miniatures for the opening title sequence to One from the Heart. Included among the shots was a complex move on the Golden Nugget sign in miniature, passed through it traveled down, transitioning to a scene on the live-action street set. / Joystick in hand, Tom Hollister tests a move on the motion control system. Working with effects which the camera floated around the large-scale from behind and then 56 - CINEFEX 12 standard linear rails everybody uses,\" said Tom Hollister. \"The belting system to move the rig along the track is a pre-stretched aircraft cable made of stainless steel with a neoprene coating - a ladder belt. There's no capstan drive, so there's no possibility of slippage. When you reverse direction, you can tell if there's a change of 10,000ths of an inch.\" With sixteen-foot main tracks, and a few eight-foot extensions and cross-pieces, the motion control 'erector set\" components make unlimited configurations possible is very limited stage area. As in most other effects facilities, the Dream Quest stages are painted flat black to minimize bounce when photographing models in low-light situations. Stage 1, the graphics stage, has a single sixteen-foot main track with a Mitchell Standard GC camera, and the model rig moves both north-south and eastwest. The eight-axis controller moves the rig, articulates the models and runs the camera's animation motor on most of their closeup and intermediate model shots, graphics and stopmotion work. For any graphics overflow and fast rotoscoping, in a tiny room adjacent to the short stage is Dream Quest's homemade version of an Oxberry - dubbed the \"Dingleberry\" - a primitive down-shooter which can be run manually or with another of their floating controllers. On the larger Stage 2, the model remains more stationary, with the moves accomplished mainly through the pan and tilt of the other Mitchell military camera on their sixteen-axis controller. A storage area is being converted to a third shooting stage to handle the excess workload and otherwise extra \"erector set\" modules, parts and pieces. Between shop and storage is Rocco Gioffre's matte room, where the original matte stand from their garage/studio is still in almost daily operation. \"Heck, I don't need much to do a lot,\" said Gioffre. \"With a one-day notice and a one-week deadline, I created a tornado for Little House on the Prairie. It's really too bad that more producers and directors don't employ mattes to solve more of their problems. After all this time, I still don't think « all the possible matte painting applications have even been scratched.\" If matte painting is the simple problem solver, Dream Quest's computers tackle the truly impossible tasks, making moves no human could ever duplicate. Fred Iguchi - assisted by his wife, Kathy - builds the company's computers. \"We chose what we thought were the best basic designs, then modified them with some timing subleties I don't think anyone else has thought of. We designed the hardware so it would be as easy as possible to operate; then Scott really concentrated on what the software should look like.\" As much as possible, Dream Quest buys their computer components off-the-shelf from California Computer Systems, Northstar, Morro Designs, and Digital Equipment Corporation.\" We've also bought a traveling follow-focus system from Greg McMurry's Microlab company. Why build anything ourselves if someone's already got it? We're able to buy very powerful computer components now, plus quad-density floppy disc controllers with a direct memory access, and memory boards with 256 kilobytes of RAM. The only boards we now build ourselves are the actual computer-to-motor interfaces. For these, we've had Jim Goodnight generate printed circuit board Motion control systems in use throughout Hollywood run on a variety of different languages. Dream Quest runs a hybrid system. All the number-crunching, data-creating software has been written in BASIC, while assembly language is used for the actual on-line shooting system. Squires writes the software that creates all the programs for motion control, and is in the process of writing a second-generation system in an entirely different language which will give Dream Quest the ability to do live-action, real-time motion-control moves on stage and on location. The last workspace in this Escher-like labyrinth is a small room housing Scott Squires' graphics terminal. The setup is one a medical doctor might recognize - IDS printer, DEC graphics terminal, TEI microframe mainframe, and GTCO digitizing pad - equipment used for analysis of x-rays. Squires uses the digitizing pad in two basic ways. \"It's translucent, like a process screen, so you can draw a flightpath or any freehand field chart on paper, then digitize each of those points to translate them directly into the computer; or we can put the pad up in the frame and project our target using Hoyt's process projector. In dealing with the oddball-type inputs - like the graphics terminals and the digitizers - they're all semi-intelligent, so I am working on making their responses standard in relation to the rest of our computer systems.\" There is a formidable measure of magic compressed into Dream Quest's modest 10,000-square-foot facility, where these young wizards wage war against time. They have had steady employment from a growing client list of producers, directors, editors and supervisors, with projects like Blade Runner and Apogee's Death and the Devil's Gorge, as well as numerous commercials for such clients as Motor Week, Kawasaki, Astroworld, Northwest Orient, even Maybelline. Perhaps the most significant commercial for Dream Quest was a stylish Lincoln Continental ad brought to them by Robert Swarthe. The client had rented a massive soundstage at M-G-M and built a surrealistic landscape with a silver-metallic roadway stretching off to a distant horizon. \"The automobile was parked there beneath a huge, forty-foot diameter, black arch,\" said Swarthe. \"At the back of the stage hung an eerie sunsetcloudscape backdrop. In the foreground, there was a man and woman in formal attire. He hands her a diamond necklace. The diamonds fly out of her hand and down the roadway towards the arch. They form a semicircle beneath it and become the letters C-O-N-T-I-N-E-N-T-A-L. Finally, the arch and letters matchdissolve flawlessly to the Continental logo on the trunk of the car. The camera had to dolly and tilt during that shot, so I knew it would be impossible to match the scenes without motion control.\" The director wanted low, wide angles; but with a 17mm lens, they were getting fifty degrees more than they wanted - including the catwalks and rafters. That's when I quietly asked Rocco if he was ready to do an emergency glass shot,\" Swarthe continued.i We ended up doing two glass shots. They set up and Gioffre painted all night. They only had time to shoot one test to"],["57","Dream Quest","CINEFEX 12 • 57 GAMBLING HALL GOLDEN NUGGET GAMBLING HALL 77VA"],["58","Dream Quest","58 - CINEFEX 12"],["59","Dream Quest","check their depth-of-field and how the painting blended with the set. The test was good. The scene was shot the next day and it was perfect.\" To prepare for the pull-back from the trunk of the car, Dream Quest came in at night, while the set was closed and the crew asleep, and spent all night setting up their motion control system - tracks, beds, camera and controller. Tom Hollister had to align and stabilize the delicate system. \"The main crew came in at 7 a.m. to light the all-black Lincoln. We shot a test move, ran the film back to our facility for processing, and had it back in their hands within an hour. The pull-back needed a delicate little north-south tilt so the back of the car perfectly matched the predetermined horizon line all through the move. You couldn't have done that with the world's best cameraman and seven hunFor Dream Quest, it was seventy-two hours - nonstop - from the time they came on the set until the time they left; and for the director and crew, not a moment of production time was lost due to special effects. The flying diamonds took another fourteen days to complete back at the Dream Quest facility. \"We had fourteen diamonds on the first flyby,\" Swarthe explained, \"one for each letter in the logo and three more to add some variety. Each had its own separate flight path to make them look like fourteen independently flying jewels. They were shot one at a time, with three or four combined on a single negative so that in the optical compositing we only had to matte three layers of film over the background instead of fourteen.\" The commercial became one of the most successful in the history of marketing automobiles. Convinced that Dream Quest was capable of anything he might ask of them, Bob Swarthe employed them once again for some matte work and to shoot the critically praised opening title sequence for One from the Heart (11:67-68). It seemed like Dream Quest was destined to do better work for even more challenging Little did they realize how good it would get, or how fast. After their work in the Lincoln Continental ad, Dream Quest was retained to do a single sequence for E.T. \"I've known Scott and Hoyt for a long time,\" said E.T. effects supervisor Dennis Muren, who contacted them on behalf of ILM, \"since Scott was my assistant on the mothership photography for Close Encounters. I'd been following what they were doing, and this was the first chance to do something together. It was the scene where E.T. performed his first 'miracle,' levitating the clay balls in a representation of the solar system. What you're seeing there is the same ball shot five different times, each one a double pass for front- and backlighting. They shot probably five different versions relatively quickly, and we were able to conduct all our discussions lonq-distance.\" Hoyt Yeatman described the sequence of development. \"We remade the balls to two-thirds their original size so we could scale the move down in motion control. We made them a marbled but neutral color, so ILM could colorize them later, but Dennis still wanted a light effect added as the balls came up off the table and passed from the light of the desk lamp into the ambient room light. To do that, we devised a system of flags and filters that would rotate in front of the lens to change the lighting effect. We would shoot some tests; they'd send a driver out to pick up the tests and airship them to Marin County; then Spielberg and Muren would call with their comments. This transpired over a four-week period, but we didn't get to see the final effect until the movie was released. The most interesting aspect was our infinite choice of individual orbit combinations. We were even allowed some creative decisions, as long as it tied in with the live-action - when the actors looked up, the balls had to follow their eye movements instead of the other way around.\" Right down to the little moments that had been subcontracted, E.T. proved to be a labor of love created by mutual friends. Yeatman spoke for his partners. \"It was both a vote of confidence and an incredibly nice gesture for ILM and Apogee and Trumbull to use our services on their projects - 'cause if we let them down, they were the ones who would have to take the heatiAfter two years of effective service, doing piecework for every conceivable type of effect, Dream Quest felt ready to \"take the heat\" themselves. They were prepared to wait for the right vehicle; but no sooner was the wish made than it was granted, and Dream Quest embarked on its first solo effort in a major moBlue Thunder is a Columbia release of a Rastar/ Gordon Carroll production, directed by John Badham whose previous credits include Saturday Night Fever, Dracula and Whose Life Is It Anyway? It is the story of Vietnam vet Roy Scheider, now a chopper pilot for the Los Angeles Police Department, who is chosen to test-fly the latest spy weapon in America's military/political arsenal - the Blue Thunder helicopter. Despite the usual flying bullets and spectacular pyrotechnics, Blue Thunder is not to be considered a typical special effects film. It is set in present-day Los Angeles, shot on location using a real helicopter in the title role - a French Aerospatiale Gazelle converted into a convincing experimental attack vehicle. In fact, in this realistic contemporary drama there was no need to fake a thing until the extended chase sequence in the last reel, when Scheider steals the killer 'copter and takes on airborne SWAT units and some F-16 jet fighters in the skies over Los Angeles. For some of these scenes, the filmmakers found it necessary to turn to the tools of fantasy in order to duplicate reality.iWe got a lot of cooperation from big corporations,\" John Badham explained, \"getting all kinds of electronic and navigational equipment for free or for rental. But we got a flat refusal from the military and an 'official' refusal from the Los Angeles Police - they didn't want 'LAPD' on the side of our helicopters, yet the police were very nice and helpful with advice on behavior and police procedure. But the Air Force didn't approve of our script from a public relations standpoint, and we had to have those F-16s for the final action sequences. The chase is dramatically integral to the script; and this particular section, where we needed about twenty motion control shots, had been in there since I read the first draft. We kept hoping that we would find a way to do it without special effects. There was even a suggestion that we go to Israel and paint some Dream Quest's first solo assignment on a feature film involved producing - for Blue Thunder - about two dozen shots of miniature F-16s flying low-level over downtown Los Angeles. Flawless insertion of miniatures into daylight skies has always been a special effects nightmare, but Dream Quest developed a technique to overcome it. Working closely with optical technicians at Cinema Research, they delivered a series of highly kinetic shots with realistic motion blur - and no matte lines. / Hoyt Yeatman programs a model move. / An F-16 - flat-lit with ultraviolet light - undergoes a matte pass on the Dream Quest stage. CINEFEX 12 • 59"],["60","Dream Quest","An ultraviolet-sensitive paint illuminating during the matte pass. Photographed on color against-black image can be used to optically generate the Squires takes a light reading console - is responsible for makes the model selfstock, the resulting redrequired mattes. / Scott off the F-16. / Squires - seated at his computer all the motion control software utilized at Dream Quest. 60 • CINEFEX 12 of their jets; but then we would have had shots of real jets in empty air. That's nothing like the shots we have now with jets in the L.A. airspace - where you can see the city in the bottom of the frame and jets in the top of the frame and they are close, working together terrifically well.\" Badham is no stranger to the special requirements of effects work. \"Every one of my films has had very elaborate special effects. You may not be aware of them - and effects aren't very good if you do notice them - but in the past I've used matte paintings, front projection, rear projection, bluescreen and all the mechanical techniques that are standard. But this was the first time I'd used motion control, so we went to ILM, which we perceived to be the best. We got our hearts stopped when we found out how expensive it was, then started looking around to see if anybody could do it for less. Trumbull was busy with Brainstorm and Dykstra was doing Firefox, so we wondered •.just who are the other guys?\" Columbia asked veteran effects man Larry Butler - Academy Award-winner for the 1940 Korda version of Thief of Bagdad - to evaluate the current talents and techniques available in the Hollywood area, and Dream Quest proved a likely candidate. \"Mr. Butler came to our facility,\" Scott Squires explained, \"saw our demo reel and seemed fairly impressed, even though our reel shows a lot more special effects than realistic simulations. So we got into discussions with Columbia and agreed to do a demo shot of exactly what they wanted - F-16s flying over Los Angeles. We shot a plate of the Arco Towers, and then had two off-the-shelf model F-16s swoop behind the buildings and fly straight towards the camera. It was a six- or seven-second shot with minimal matting problems. The planes flew the way they should, so Columbia paid a token fee for the shot - a price that didn't really cover the cost of materials. Then we had to try and figure out how much each shot would really cost. \"The most difficult aspect in this business is not doing the shots - it's budgeting them in advance on work which requires a certain amount of research and development, and guesswork on how many times the client will change his mind. They always tell you that the storyboards are their Bible - but we are all sinners. We couldn't get storyboards from Columbia, or even the number of shots they wanted. Yet, typically, they wanted a quote from us that they could 'live with.' So we gave a realistic bid for one shotiThat fixed bid almost cost Dream Quest the entire project when the client assumed that the price of the one shot times the total number of shots would equal Dream Quest's quote. One Columbia executive's opposition to dealing with what he considered \"kids\" also complicated the matter. \"He demanded to deal with 'somebody older,\" Scott Squires recalled. \"So Tom Hollister (who is bearded and at least looks older) delivered the 'one-shot' bid. Without any discussion, without reading the text, their eyes went to the bottom-line number. The executive literally threw the papers in the air, saidiNo way!,' and asked Tom to leave the meeting. We didn't hear from them for months.\" Gregg Champion, associate producer on Blue Thunder, confirmed Columbia's dissatisfaction. It was a real knee-jerk reaction on our part. We blanched when we saw the price and immediately, without really negotiating with Dream Quest, we went to another company and found out the hard way what a mistake that was.\" Badham, well-aware of the need for a leadtime on special effects, and satisfied with Dream Quest's reputation, knew that their bid for a real shot was bound to be higher than the test shot fee. Nevertheless, he was surprised at the price difference, and even more surprised to find strangers doing shots with his first and second units. \"I'm in the middle of the start of shooting the movie and Columbia goes out and finds somebody who will deliver shots at a bargain-basement price. It turns out this outfit didn't even own any motion control equipment, so it took months before Gregg could see their first shot. Gregg came back, shaking his head. He said flatly: 'It's that bad!' Gregg and Phil Feldman, our executive producer, went and tried to get together with these fellows, until sanity prevailed. With everyone here still squawking and screaming, we managed to persuade everybody to go back to Dream Quest before matters got worse. The special effects on Blue Thunder took us longer than necessary, and cost more than necessary, due to pure bureaucratic bungling.\" Badham was sorry, too, about the age prejudice shown in prior negotiations, and was certain that none of his staff was responsible. Jet Mouth and The Whiz Kids? I don't care if they come in in diapers! Just as long as they deliver.\" Dream Quest delivered. They got together with Peter Donen, who was handling the Blue Thunder optical work at Cinema Research, and devised a time-and-materials bid - a different way to look at the same dollars while the price remained the same. Gregg Champion dealt directly with Dream Quest at this juncture, and was quite impressed with their attitude.i\"They never postured. We were late in coming back to them, but they never said: 'Okay, now we got you by the balls and it's going to cost you twice as much.' They not only devised newer ways of doing things for us, but also worked out a more cost-effective way After two years of piecework, Hoyt Yeatman explained why the Blue Thunder project was so important to Dream Quest. This was to be the first feature film done on our own, and we were fortunate to get an intermediate-sized project - with twenty or thirty shots - before we try to tackle one with a hundred or more. This was our first test as a team.\" The basic task for Dream Quest was to put model F-16 fighter jets in low-altitude combat over downtown Los Angeles, where the FAA forbade fixed-wing aircraft to fly. The problems had started even before Dream Quest came aboard. Yeatman unveiled the first dilemma.\"iMost of the scenes had been predesigned and we were just following them out. They had already shot miles and miles of background plates - flat, 'Scope, VistaVision - and none of the plates made any sense in relation to their storyboards; nor did they make any technical sense in terms of being able to matte anything in them. We were able to use a couple of those early shots, but most of the downtown stuff had to be reshot right away.\" \"We also supervised the shooting of the aerial plates,\" said"],["61","Dream Quest","CINFFEX 12 • 61"],["62","Dream Quest","62 - CINEFEX 12 02"],["63","Dream Quest","Dream Quest business manager Keith Shartle. \"The storyboards called for shots which would have to be filmed from the side of a helicopter, but which would need to have the feeling of going about three hundred miles per hour. To suggest that kind of speed, we knew that we'd have to shoot in the neighborhood of six frames per second. As a result, an eight-second cut would require thirty-two seconds of stable flight - which is nearly impossible for a helicopter at the altitude and path required. But we had to go with a helicopter because no fixed-wing aircraft can be flown at low altitudes over Los Angeles. Hoyt, working in conjunction with aerial cameraman Robert Eberlein, suggested the use of a Tyler boat gyro which had been designed to stabilize the horizon when shooting on water. But stabilizing the horizon proved not to be enough, since helicopters tend to fly in a zigzag pattern as opposed to a straight line. It was necessary, therefore, to be able to correct a third axis to prevent what on the screen would appear to be a slight back-and-forth panning motion of the camera. Nelson Tyler agreed to design and build a third gyro to work with the existing boat gyro setup. This combination proved successful, and we were able to produce smooth footage out the side door of the helicopter at very low frame rates.\" Obtaining adequate background plates was only part of the problem, however. Before model photography could commence, Scott Squires had to write special programming to deal with the i\"We were using straight 35mm problem of anamorphics. squeezed, which is not the ultimate in image quality and resolution. Anamorphics, unlike sperical lenses, cannot be bellows mounted or mounted on a traditional follow-focus base. We had to rig a special gear device which literally rotated the lens barrel - like an assistant would do in live-action - which meant that all our focus curves had to be different for each lens. The lenses are difficult to view through when the image is squeezed 2.35:1, and they don't squeeze equally through the field. With the distortion in a 25mm anamorphic, a straight horizontal line makes a continuous curve through the lens - which means that, on a left-to-right flyby, as the model jet enters the frame it looks as if the nose is digging into the ground; then it tilts up as it moves across. \" Squires' computations in the motion control program turned each twisted image into smooth and level simulation of flight. The models themselves presented migrain-producing problems. \"They were off-the-shelf kits of F-16 fighters,\" Yeatman groaned. \"They were only eighteen inches in length and, just like on the real jets, they had domed canopies which acted just like fisheye lenses, reflecting our whole lighting arrangement. Eighteen inches is just too small for closeups - when you have to be three inches from the model - but most of the shots were pretty straightforward and fast enough that the minimum of detailing was acceptable.\" When the military denied Badham and Columbia access to military equipment and locations, Dream Quest took the opportunity to invent a new shot to begin the F-16 sequence. \"Rather than use some research footage of a takeoff,\" said Yeatman, \"we suggested it could be more interesting than the original lockedOnly the F-16 footage was produced by Dream Quest. The \"star\" helicopter was a custom-modified Aerospatiale Gazelle. / In addition to straight miniature work, Dream Quest photographed most of the plates into which their footage would be incorporated. Perched on a platform in downtown Los Angeles, Hoyt Yeatman photographs a locked-off plate - for a shot eventually cut from the film - as the Blue Thunder helicopter flies by. / For several scenes, miniature jets were matted into background plates, with those composites in turn being used as bluescreen plates for foreground action in a mockup cockpit. / From a distant rooftop, Yeatman and Robert Hollister photograph the Los Angeles skyline for a scene in which the F-16s fly past camera and over the city. / For scenes of a Sidewinder missile in flight, aerial plate photography was accomplished with a custom gyrostabilized camera mount. • 63 CINEFEX 12"],["64","Dream Quest","In addition to motion control photography on the F-16s, Dream Quest also produced footage of the aircraft's Sidewinder missile which is fired at the hijacked Blue Thunder helicopter. Keith Shartle affixes a small-scale missile to the miniature aircraft. / Closeups and inflight shots were achieved with a larger-scale version, being adjusted on its mount by Tom Hollister. 64 * CINEFEX 12 off shot that was planned if we could move the camera, copying a move from the original military footage we were studying. John Badham agreed that this would be a very nice start to the F-16 sequence, using our Van Nuys location. We did it by the seat of our pants, only this time we were using a very long lens - about a 200mm. It was nearly impossible to look through the lens and imagine the trajectory of the ships, so Tom Hollister built a 'debumper' - a simple but ingenious device something like a French curve. We had an O'Conner fluid head, and extended the panning arm off the tripod about four feet with a tube. This arm rested on a piece of PVC pipe. Every eight inches we drilled small, quarter-inch diameter holes in the PVC and put threaded rods through each hole. We then could bend this pipe like a French curve and sweep the extended tripod arm across the desired trajectory without even having to look through the camera. It took only twenty minutes to adjust this for the proper angle; then we mounted the whole rig in the back of a sixteen-foot Ryder van, pulled right up to the airport, opened the doors and starting shooting. Because the bumps really show with a long lens, we shot a number of takes, with the 96 frames-per-second one being the one that was finally approved. We made a registered print from that and basically roto'd up that print on the graphics stage, working between and around the model closeups being shot there at the same time. We made a black-and-white composite of both the background and the ship move; then that was again roto'd and Rocco traced a 2-D cardboard shadow in blackand-white for a separate move to be introduced later in A considerably different approach was required for the basic in-flight scenes which comprised the bulk of the Dream Quest workload.iMost of the effects shots have the jets moving in deep perspective,\" Yeatman continued. \"They fly from a pinpoint 'til they're just scraping over the top of the lens at approximately three hundred miles per hour. When they reach the camera, in the last two or three frames they become nothing but a blur. That presents a lot of optical problems way down the line, trying to matte a blur over a light-colored background. The procedure we used was basically a front- and backlighted technique with 5235 stock, which has the ability to have a continuous tone and see the streaks that we get in those last frames. The main trick was to get the mattes just dense enough so that they didn't become transparent, and yet maintain that streak on it. The amount of light we were using was around sixty-four footcandles for the key and thirty-eight to forty-two footcandles for the fill. When you dupe something in the optical stage, the contrast really grows. So when you're outdoors, in a bright and sunny scene with sky all around, you can't have any really black blacks - it looks very artificial. So everything has to be filled. Our shutter speeds were around four to eight seconds per frame, so that meant approximately sixteen seconds per frame that goes through the camera with a continuous shutter, and the reason for this is because of the speeds. Traveling eight to ten inches per frame on the track, in order to maintain the resolution that we need in terms of repeatability, we had to keep the linear speed of the track slow enough to prevent the inertia of the mechanism from causing oscillation, vibration, jiggle or sway.\" Model photography's problems certainly didn't end with lighting. \"When aircraft are flying along side-by-side and the pilot looks out of his cockpit and sees his buddy flying alongside, it doesn't matter if you've got a stabilized camera or not. There's going to be some change of pan and tilt - angular displacement - and when they do process work, either in front projection or rear projection, where they hang models on wires or with any other suspension, the model is always doing something completely different and independent from what the background is doing. So one of the things we got into that worked quite well was to digitize the helicopter background plates. There is a navigational system which will supposedly record the altitude changes of an aircraft in flight, which can get very complex; but our approach was that we didn't care what the camera helicopter did while it was flying. Instead, we concentrated on what ended up on the film. When we got our hands on the aerial footage, we would take a registered black-and-white print and put it in a process projector, then throw it on a twenty-by-twenty digitizing pad like a translucent rear projection screen. When we began the session, we would put a twelve-field animation chart in the projector, take a little digitizing meter with crosshairs on it and designate 'zero' in the center of the screen, then pick an object so many fields over. This is to calibrate the machine, telling the computer where it starts from. Then you roll down the film footage until the part where you want to start putting the airplane in. Next you pick some point on the distant horizon - the furthest point you can clearly see, such as an antenna - and you put the cross-hairs right over that point and hit the button. The computer takes the x-y coordinates of that point and automatically advances the projector to the next frame. Of course, that point has now moved; so you repeat the process, which is like rotoscoping, but with a computer which gives you a list of the x-y positions of angular displacement from frame-to-frame. When we got a normal, standard model move approved, then we would take that information and superimpose all the jiggles and bumps into the software program. If you were looking at the final move on a grid, it would look very jerky; but when it's matted with the jerky movements in, it matches!\" To get a feeling of true atmosphere in the deep perspective shots, adding smoke or diffusion did not produce the desired effect: so Dream Quest worked closely with Peter Donen at Cinema Research, optically dissolving the various film elements as the aircraft flew into the distance. Gregg Champion had direct responsibility in the delicate optical work.iPosed with the challenge of making F-16s look absolutely real, over a city everybody knows, is a much more difficult problem to solve than any kind of fantasy setting where a little imbalance might be acceptable. Ideally, Dream Quest should do their own compositing, but Cinema Research did an absolutely excellent job for us on the opticals.\" Cinema Research is one of Hollywood's busiest optical houses, doing an endless succession of graphics and commercials, titles"],["65","Dream Quest","and split-screens - and delicate little composites for a very demanding Dream Quest. \"Little is right!\" exclaimed Peter Donen. 'Fifty feet at a time...twenty feet...ten feet...sometimes three feet at a timel The stuff we tend to get into with Dream Quest is generally more demanding in terms of the amount of thought and experimentation. It seems every time we do a shot with them, it's fun; but we're always doing something different than it's ever been done before.\" Donen was particularly enthusiastic about the new technique in model photography that was developed during the Blue Thunder project. \"I feel proud just to have been a part of the solution to the problem that has plagued effects people - how to get believable fast motion in a frame, and consequently keep costs fairly under control. It still isn't easy, but it is now easier to do a large number of shots where model airplanes fly like real airplanes and look real - without strobing or wrap-around problems or big holes in your mattes from bluescreen reflections. Models now have all the dynamic action of the real McCoy, and it was the kids at Dream Quest who took an old idea and actually made it work! I'm their biggest fan.\" While the airplane model photography settled into a routine, Dream Quest faced considerable difficulties in creating satisfactory vapor trails for the deadly Sidewinder missile that an F-16 was to launch at the Blue Thunder helicopter.iThe missile trails were actually done in a number of ways,\" Yeatman explained. We couldn't use CO2 on the forward and away shots because there were problems with density build-up. Basically, it just goes to black. That's the biggest problem in this area - just trying to get a wispy trail. So, depending on the angle of each particular shot, we used liquid carbon dioxide or liquid nitrogen, which gave us a vapor trail about one-and-a-half to two body-lengths long. However, the stuff was so cold that it would cause the paint to crack on the missile and the fins would fall off; so we had our Sidewinder model made with two set-screws so the whole body of the missile could slide right off - leaving just the exhaust system, which we'd then photographed in a separate pass. Working at stop-motion speeds of four- to eight-second exposures turned the CO, into a smooth, soft blur. So we connected strobes to the shutter and at the appropriate time they fired, resulting in this nice blend of strobed and ambient light. Strobes alone would be too...stroby - which looks too animated. But with a low-density fill light always on, you get the feeling of blurring but the clouds are still sharply defined. Most of the left-to-right shots use this strobed CO, technique.\" There was one more \"cheat\" on the Sidewinder shots. \"In real life, they reach Mach 2 at sixty feet, so you'd never even see them. In order to make it exciting - and visible - on the screen, shots of the missile were scaled down to about five hundred miles per hour.\" Using a thirty-inch scale model of the Sidewinder, with a large comparably-scaled F-16 wing section, the majority of the missile te meter stages shots were also reduced to simple flybys on the model stages. But there was one critical missile shot that taxed the imagination and skills of the Dream Quest team. At this point in the chase, the F-16s have cornered the Blue Thunder helicopter and are zeroing in for the kill. Scheider hides the helicopter in a CINEFEX 12 • 65"],["66","Dream Quest","66 - CINEFEX 12"],["67","Dream Quest","blinding solar reflection off the mirrored windows of the Arco Towers in downtown Los Angeles, and then veers away as the heat-seeking Sidewinder goes for the sun's image instead of the chopper. Director Badham knew how difficult the shot was, and was willing to do it until it was perfect. \"With this kind of action, it's no good to do it with a fixed-camera shot. But we weren't getting anything good with a missile strapped underneath our camera 'copter because it would only fly at eighty or ninety knots; and when you undercrank that to give it the feeling of five hundred to six hundred knots, all you get is shake. Instead, we did some pure point-of-view - with no missile - flying as fast as we could at the Towers. Then it was up to Dream Quest to put the missile in the shot, track it frame-by-frame and correct it for what the camera 'copter was doing. It was not locked-off, so I knew it was going to be extraordinarily complicated and a tremendous problem...for them!\" Hoyt Yeatman was aboard on the last day of shooting the wild background plates. Flying out of \"Piper Tech\" - an LAPD staging area for their own aircraft - they tried to get a useful \"missile POV\" shot. Useful and exciting, as Yeatman found out. \"In previous takes, we had flown in as close as possible to the Tower and then flown up-and-over the building at the last second. This day, our stunt pilot, Rick Holley, had gotten a special clearance to come down 'just a little different' on Jim Gavin, who was flying the Blue Thunder helicopter. He flew in even closer than before, but instead of going up he flipped our 'copter on its side and flew between the buildings!\" Gregg Champion was also on board, monitoring the video playback of each take.i\"We ended up doing seventeen passes at the Acro Towers - and each time, we could feel the standing air pocket when we got close enough to see paperclips on the desks in the offices. We tested every rate, and what we finally ended up using was cranked at six frames per second.\" When this footage was shown during the dailies, it was so spectacular everyone was leaning back in their seats in anticipation of impact. Badham was ecstatic until Yeatman expressed some doubt about the possibility of adding the critical reflection to the checkerboard windows of the Tower. \"I really thought it was impossible, but Mr. Badham said: 'Okay. If you don't put that sun reflection in... well, I'll just have to stop the film in the middle of the chase, wipe to my face and tell the audience, \"Well this was going to be a really neat scene, but the guys at Dream Quest said it couldn't be done; so now I'll describe it for those of you still in the theater...\" ' We got his point.\" The Dream Quest team went back to work with a vengeance. iWe experimented for two days before we found we were able to track all those wild movements on our in-house graphics system,\" Yeatman admitted. \"It worked just like an animation stand on its side - with an east-west of eight feet and a northsouth of six, perpendicular to the camera track. We put up a cross-hair target where the lightbox would be, and frame-byframe we projected through the camera, rotoscoping the footage from our 'copter camera. There was a particular window in the Tower we wanted the reflection to be coming from, so we moved the x-y-and-z axis of the camera to lock the cross-hairs on that window. After a period of two hundred frames or so, we got that done and our computer knew all the points for each frame. With the main problem solved, Scott went in with the smoothing function and ironed out all the little 'in-betweens.' The glint is made up of basically two passes on the same light source - the first with diffusion filters to give a general sheen to the face of the building; then that is back-wound, the filters removed, and we shoot the hot-point to give it a nice, intense kick in the lens. I took a low-density black-and-white matte of all those little windows, and used as a point source a little ENH slide projector bulb - a very high-intensity quartz light with internal reflector. Projecting it through a pin-hole gave us a very intense collimated light beam for the kick; and by projecting that right into the lens with some diffusion, it spread out into a nice glow. We did several tests in black-and-white, playing around to get the combination that looked best. Then we did our first test run onto 5247 color negative. Cinema Research put it together for us and we found that when you dupe a flare you loose all its initial qualities; so we switched to 5243, which was the stock they were compositing on. It's an extremely slow intermediate stock, so to get the natural blurring we were after - and because we were shooting continuous-shutter, we got up to about four minutes exposure per frame. This four-second finished shot took about eight hours of camera time. Finally, in order to get the desirable, delicate qualities of a flare, we had to do what the Doug Trumbull group does quite a bit. Cinema Research would take the missile shot, the smoke trail shot and the background shot, composite the scene and hold it latent. Then we would bring that over to our cameras, bipack the latent with the black-and-white version of the background, and use the ENH bulb as a printing source, exposing the flares onto the building, with the computer tracking the whole move. You know, a sun reflection off a building is made up of a lot of little squares, so as an extra realistic touch we timed the flare to blink or pulse as the Blue Thunder helicopter flew in front of our point of reflection. John Badham liked this shot so much that they added several more shots where they wanted this sunset effect; but because of several subtle differences in each one, we wound up shooting three of them on 5243 and then went back to 5247 for the other two.\" Scott Squires had a wry observation about their client's faith in Dream Quest's creative abilities. 'We made the 'mistake' of impressing them with our speed and quality; so they were inspired to expand our workload, but couldn't extend the deadline proportionately. For instance, when we added that glare to the first shot, it made them want to see a sunkick in the other shots where the building appears. They got a little crazy on those flares; but fortunately, John Badham is a very level-headed man who actually understands the problems we face. Many other directors have tried to make things 'easier' for us, not realizing that even a little change in the way something moves can often complicate matters - immensely. Many times clients will 'fish' and wait for something to come up that they like, but Badham had the rare Hoyt Yeatman makes lighting adjustments on the largescale missile and partial F-16 wing section for a scene of the Sidewinder being fired. / In-flight shots of the missile were photographed using Dream Quest's standard motion control techniques. / Exhaust trails - either liquid carbon dioxide or liquid nitrogen - were photographed on a separate pass. Since the frigid temperatures generated by the compressed gases caused cracks and paint flaking on the model, the Sidewinder was constructed so its outer shell could be removed during the exhaust pass. CINEFEX 12 • 67"],["68","Dream Quest","Rocco Gioffre studies art department concepts for a National Lampoon's Vacation matte painting. / Plates for the original negative matte shot were photographed in the Santa Anita racetrack parking lot. The two-by-four at the top provided support for the matte - not yet in place. / Gioffre positions his completed painting in the Dream Quest matte stand. / In the final shot, actor Chevy Chase and his movie son run toward the entrance to the fictitious WalleyWorld amusement park. 68 • CINEFEX 12 Badham definitely wanted another reflection added to the shot when the missile actually strikes the high-rise office building, so the Dream Quest principals were invited out to the San Fernando Valley to consult on certain camera angles. Columbia had built an eighty-foot-high miniature of the upper-story sections of the Arco Towers, rigged to explode when Dream Quest's Sidewinder was supposed to hit. A radio-controlled, tenth-scale model of the Blue Thunder helicopter was to hover in front of the Tower and then zip away at the moment of the explosion. Yeatman recalled the tension that extended over two days. \"This was Badham's most expensive split-second decision in the whole feature. They had eight cameras running anywhere from twenty-four to five hundred frames per second, with the camera operators poised to throw their switches and run like hell. John Simone, who built the models, spent two days operating the fantastically detailed helicopter while suspended in a cherry-picker about fifty feet in the air. As experienced as John is, radio control is real squirrelly and it was hard for him to get the model to go through the frame just right. The first one crashed into a pipe rig the first day: but he had a spare, which they couldn't fly 'til the next day. With enough fuel for three passes, everyone held their breath and committed on the second pass. It was perfect. About four or five guys detonated the building manually in sequence - with an explosion first in the window, then a fireball, then a mortar to shoot out all these doll furniture parts. It blew out about ten stories, and I guess the explosion interfered with the radio control, because the second model went behind the building and crashed in the parking lot - five feet from a Rolls Royce, as the After a little more sweetening on a couple of the F-16 shots, Dream Quest's first solo feature film project was wrapped. Keith Shartle outlined the statistics, sans figures: \"We were originally contracted to do twenty-four shots. At one time it grew to as many as thirty, then was cut back to twenty-seven final production shots, a few of which have been cut from the finished film. We worked on the project for 141 shooting days on 311 different pieces of film. We shot 165 different move and tracking tests in black-and-white, and seventy-eight different color tests for such things as missile smoke, aircraft bodies and so forth. We had sixty-eight total production shoots - a shoot generally being one airplane and its matte pass or a missile and its pass. We shot 123 actual different elements - with the average shot taking five shooting days, with an average of four elements or passes for each shot, and about six move tests. Every shooting day we averaged two pieces of film being shot on two stages. We went through four thousand feet of color stock; black-and-white was about twice that. With backgrounds, it came to about twenty thousand feet of film for our work on those twenty-odd shots. The shortest shot is thirty frames, or just over a second on the screen; and the longest is just over nine-and-a-half seconds. By contrast, the editor on Blue Thunder told me he had had his hands on over 350,000 feet of film, not including the VistaVision plates. For the amount of action in this picture, I'm not surprised. It's the most incredible aerial work I've ever seen.\" First times are learning times. What did this experience teach the folks at Dream Quest? \"A lot about lighting, quite a bit about the matting process, hints and techniques to streamline and accelerate the entire stage process - and quite a bit about the bidding process for features versus commercials. We have always delivered what we promised on time and within budget; and so far, we have never gone over budget on any project, although the dollars might be slim. From Blue Thunder, the principal payoff was experience, which means future clients will benefit from more productivity. We are happy with the results, the client is happy, and we have already had some referrals from various people at Columbia who have recommended us 'without qualification.'\" There is still one major hurdle for Dream Quest to get over - age. While youth is a precious commodity in other aspects of the entertainment business, it can be an enemy when the young men of Dream Quest try to conduct business. \"We get along fine with other technical people, but business people just don't seem to want to deal with 'kids.' We became involved with Close Encounters before most of us had turned twenty; so now, in our mid-twenties, we have the acknowledged expertise and our prices are very reasonable. But when negotiations go sour, we always suspect that someone else didn't trust the 'kids' with x-amount of dollars and responsibility. It's the one problem we can t outgrow fast enough.\" Any timorous producer who checked Dream Quest's impeccable references would soon have the confidence to trust that team to do anything they said they could do. \"Those three kids are the greatest thing since apple pie,\" claims Peter Donen at Cinema Research.iScott knows computers so thoroughly; Hoyt has this innate sense about technical effects; and I honestly believe Rocco is going to surpass the great matte artists in time. They've all got a lot of heart, and I've never known anyone who stands behind their work like those guys do. They are the grass roots of special effectsiRobert Swarthe found their youth an asset. 'They're - I have to say it - they're clever kids. They're the only people I know that went into business the right way, with the right kind of equipment. They're careful about the jobs they take, and I just hope they stay the way they are - devilishly clever Dennis Muren at ILM remains confident in Dream Quest's future. \"I think they are sort of the forerunners of a new breed of effects persons. These guys came up from a hardwareoriented background, had the opportunity to work with Trumbull and see what other people had going around town, and saved all their pennies to build some state-of-the-art equipment. They're already developing a 'look' or style all their own, so they should stay busy for a long time.\" And John Badham, the first director to entrust a feature film to Dream Quest? \"They have been very cooperative and tried extremely hard to do what we needed, and did it in the limited amount of time available. Even when there were changes in mid-stream, they adjusted and delivered on time. We were sensitive to each others' production needs and worked as a team to keep costs down. They really"],["69","Dream Quest","CINEFEX 12 • 69 Walley Wonde"],["70","Dream Quest","70 - CINEFEX 12"],["71","Dream Quest","If youth truly is their nemesis, it is also Dream Quest's source of strength when the workload gets heavy - as it has since they wrapped Blue Thunder. In the closing months of 1982, they began working on preproduction and testing for William Friedkin's Deal of the Century - this time with fictitious F-19s for a change of pace - and have been shooting some Greg Jein spaceships for Warner Brothers' V, a four-hour alien invasion movie, and possible series pilot, for NBC. Rocco Gioffre has been doing just a bit of matte painting - one for Zoetrope's The Outsiders and another for their The Black Stallion Returns; three for National Lampoon's Vacation; one for the Joe Dante segment of the Twilight Zone feature; two for Renault's 'chasm\" commercial; and fifteen for Warner Brothers' new TV series, Wizards and Warriors, for CBS. The young men of Dream Quest don't need grey hair to think wisely about their future. Their growth continues to be carefully planned. Tom Hollister outlined their most important development for 1983: iWe are having an optical printer built for us so we can do our own in-house compositing. This will make our facility more complete, more efficient and give us better quality control over the finished project.\" The junior Hollister will also be responsible for the new third motion control stage, as well as the general upgrading of the other stages during the year. Hoyt Yeatman will float between assignments, troubleshooting individual projects while keeping his fingers crossed that some stop-motion puppet animation will come their way. He is impatient for this particular challenge. \"Hoyt often comes up with practical, common-sense solutions and innovations in his dreams,\" Squires remarked. \"At least that's what he tells us when he's sleeping on the stage.\" Fred Iguchi will use his time to construct additional motion control packages while his partners are busy on the shooting stages. \"We finished the third system as an intermediate one to float between the matte stand, the down-shooter...wherever it's needed. This fourth system is going to be used almost exclusively by the matte painting department, and will give Rocco the ability to control all axes on his existing plane, with separate channels for additional gags or effects devices he employs to add that dimensionality to his matte paintings. \"I've also started on the fifth system, which will be another wild system that's going to bounce from stage-to-stage and to locations. But it will probably end up on the third stage we are building to fit between our small sixteen-foot stage and our sixty-four-foot main stage setup. We still have to wait on parts, but basically we've got it down. We dupe our own boards, buy as much 'pre-builts' as possible and keep all the components interchangeable.\" Squires indicated that Iguchi's workload will not lessen soon. ¿'¿Our next motion control system will be a live-action system that can track a production camera move and will fit in a trunk instead of a truck. It will be very portable and very fast, with our new software in FORTH. FORTH is both a compiler-type and interpreter-type language, offering the advantages of both BASIC and Machine Code. There are advantages and disadvantages with every language, depending on the application. We wanted a system where we could create a move on a computer, and also shoot it from that same computer so we don't have to ping-pong from one room to the other while testing and refining go on. We still have the capability to split that function, but we aren't required to do so with our approach. FORTH is very modular, very fast and very quick to change. Everything is in English terms, so you can type in exactly what you want it to do. We can take any of the routines we handle, string them all together in any sequence and have just a single word to deal with it all; so the user doesn't have to be a programmer. FORTH has a lot of I/O-oriented instructions to output to ports or specific memory locations. Most languages have very minimal input and output-type words to deal with that, whereas FORTH has been set up for process control - which can update a hundred times a second, if need be. I don't want to make it sound like the ultimate do-all solution to every problem, but most languages set the rules for you, whereas with FORTH, you make the rules. There's a little God role-playing in there.\" Squires has also written custom programs for their graphics terminal so that obiects now can rotate in three dimensions. In terms of the design of motion control shots, this gives them the ability to eliminate the first two or three black-and-white motion tests on each and every shot. \"It will easily save eight to ten hours preparation for any given shot,\" Iguchi commented. Dream Quest also plans to stay on top of advanced computer graphics and look out for ways to incorporate such imagery into future productions. Indeed, Dream Quest is conservative yet consistent in their campaign to keep abreast of technical innovations applicable to the field of special effects. \"We are trying to do the R&D on our own and spread the costs,\" said Squires, who considers growth to be one of the biggest problems they will have to face in 1983. We still want to remain a tight group, but for the next large project, we will probably need more workspace, even as we try to keep it close-knit. We realize that the most important commodity we have is not space; it's the people. We are willing to enlarge, but only as long as the quality of knowledgeable people remains high.\" Three additions to the Dream Quest team are production assistants Robert Waddell, Ellie Thomas and Michael Bigelow. In any event, it seems certain that the Dream Quest principals will continue to diligently maintain the standards that united them in the first place. As Tom Hollister put it: \"Other effects houses are specialized in other ways of doing things. What we have strived for here is the ability to not specialize in anything, but to specialize in anything.\" And continue to make dreams a Marc A. Richardson is a Los Angeles-based media generalist and specialist in high-impact sound systems for concerts and nightclubs. He is currently developing The Fool, a unique musical fantasy feature film employing state-of-the-art effects technology. Alien spacecraft soar over Long Beach in V, a four-hour telefilm produced by Warner Brothers for NBC. / Robert Waddell mounts one of Greg Jein's miniature ships onto a motion-control model-mover. / Frederick Iguchi, whose prime function at Dream Quest is designing and building the various computer-control systems, also serves time on the stages. Here he loads a camera in preparation for a spaceship shot. Dream Quest produced only a portion of the miniature work in V, having to leave the project in midstream to work on the previously committed Deal of the Century. CINEFEX 12 • 71"],["72","Closing pages",""]]});
